P.transport["npc:alanndarian-nightsong"] = {
  hash = "e1fb77",
  fullHash = "b1395a",
  text = "Alanndarian Nightsong. Alanndarian Nightsong is a night elf resident of Auberdine in Darkshore dedicated to tending to the region's beleaguered fauna. She works alongside travelers to remedy the blights plaguing the local wildlife, particularly the native striders and forest creatures corrupted by dark forces. Situated in the coastal settlement of Auberdine, Alanndarian Nightsong watches over the fragile ecological balance of Darkshore. As mysterious taints seep into the soil and waters, the creatures of the ancient woods have begun to succumb to sickness and unnatural aggression. Alanndarian acts as a guardian and naturalist, seeking practical remedies to ease their suffering and restore their natural vigor. Much of Alanndarian's attention is focused on identifying the root causes of the afflictions spreading through the local fauna. Through careful gathering of botanical reagents and researching the principal water sources of the region, she crafts salves and curative draughts. She enlists wandering adventurers to apply these cures directly to ailing forest creatures, attempting to stave off the spreading corruption before it permanently overtakes Darkshore's wildlife.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_alanndarian-nightsong-c78eea84db6ab4723743/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.3885714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:althea-ebonlocke"] = {
  hash = "9ae60d",
  fullHash = "83a8ad",
  text = "Althea Ebonlocke. Commander Althea Ebonlocke leads the Night Watch, the beleaguered town guard protecting Darkshire from the terrors of Duskwood. A relative of Mayor Ello Ebonlocke, she shoulders the defense of an isolated region abandoned by Stormwind's regular military. She organizes patrols, tasks adventurers with culling local horrors, and coordinates the town's survival against constant monstrous threats. While her relative, Mayor Ello Ebonlocke, handles civil governance within the town hall, Commander Althea directs Darkshire's thin line of physical defense. When Stormwind failed to deploy royal troops to guard Duskwood against creeping horrors, the citizenry formed the Night Watch. Under Althea's steady, unyielding command, these volunteers patrol the fog-shrouded roads, keeping skeletal undead, ferocious wolves, and spiders from overrunning the town perimeter. Althea is pragmatic and understands the limits of her militia. Outnumbered and continually bleeding recruits, she freely enlists passing mercenaries and travelers to thin the threats at Darkshire's doorstep. She directs campaigns against the ghouls and skeletons at the tranquil and sorrowful graveyards of the forest, while remaining vigilant against darker rumors stirring deep in the woods.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_althea-ebonlocke-085de982e8a3f0dd4f9c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.2253061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:asterion"] = {
  hash = "145b72",
  fullHash = "af4025",
  text = "Asterion. Asterion is an ancient Highborne spirit bound to the ruins of Bashal'Aran in Darkshore. Once the trusted second to governor Athrikus Narassin ten millennia ago, he was mysteriously imprisoned in a magical moonstone seal that held him through mortal life and into the afterlife. Today, his specter remains shackled to the ruined shrine, surrounded by demonic beasts and dreaming of liberation. Before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, Bashal'Aran and Ameth'Aran were sister cities blessed by Queen Azshara herself. Azshara appointed Athrikus Narassin to govern the twin settlements, and Asterion served as his right hand. Respected for both his physical combat prowess and his command of arcane sorcery, Asterion lived among the people of Bashal'Aran while frequently traveling between the sister cities to manage the needs of their citizens. For reasons lost to time, Asterion fell out of favor with his master. Athrikus fashioned a powerful moonstone seal, forging an inescapable magical cage to confine Asterion within Bashal'Aran. The imprisonment stretched through centuries of agonizing confinement; worse still, when Asterion finally perished, he discovered death offered no release. His spirit remained bound to the ruined shrine, left behind when the world cracked open and demons overran the ancient settlement.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_asterion-87fc5046aa77a0a2d6bf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.7183673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:athridas-bearmantle"] = {
  hash = "b2434d",
  fullHash = "a9a383",
  text = "Athridas Bearmantle. Athridas Bearmantle is a night elf druid residing in Dolanaar within the boughs of Teldrassil. Tasked with safeguarding the local settlement, he directs young adventurers to investigate the encroaching corruption affecting the local wildlife and the nearby Gnarlpine furbolgs. Stationed within Dolanaar, Athridas Bearmantle stands as an early line of defense for the night elves emerging from Shadowglen. As the boughs of the newly grown world tree Teldrassil exhibit unnatural signs of decay and unrest, Athridas works tirelessly to coordinate local scouting efforts and protect the peaceful township from surrounding threats. Much of Athridas's concern centers on the neighboring Gnarlpine tribe of furbolgs. Once peaceful neighbors, the Gnarlpine have succumbed to a spreading madness and corruption that poisons their shamans and chieftains, driving them into violent frenzy against the Kaldorei. Athridas mobilizes willing champions to contain the threat and unearth the deeper taint taking root in the wilderness.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_athridas-bearmantle-d744cf17864c23c695a2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.16, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:blind-mary"] = {
  hash = "29016c",
  fullHash = "789e4f",
  text = "Blind Mary. Blind Mary is a tormented banshee haunting an abandoned, ruined farmhouse just southeast of Darkshire in Duskwood. Once a vain and beautiful mortal woman, her violent death and transformation drove her to self-inflicted mutilation in grief over what she had become. Before the shadows fell across Duskwood, Mary was known as a striking mortal woman who took immense pride in her beauty, rarely seen without her treasured looking glass. When the undead swept over the region, Mary was slain along with many of her neighbors. Unlike those who found rest in the earth, her spirit lingered on as an ethereal banshee, condemned to wander the mortal realm in a ghastly, translucent form. Awakening to undeath, Mary was overwhelmed by sorrow, horror, and fury at her monstrous reflection. Incapable of enduring the sight of her spectral visage, she clawed out her own eyes with her bare hands so she would never gaze upon herself again. In her agony, she discarded her beloved looking glass, leaving her to drift through her abandoned home as a blind, weeping wraith clutching to faint memories of the mortal life she lost.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_blind-mary-12cf07ea50dbe2c91687/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.7355102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:calor"] = {
  hash = "db2b71",
  fullHash = "c2b10f",
  text = "Calor. Calor is a dedicated resident of Darkshire who aids the Night Watch and the Carevin family in defending Duskwood. Operating under the direction of Jonathan Carevin, he actively coordinates efforts to hunt down the vicious worgen stalking the gloomy forests. Adventurers looking to push back the ravenous beasts haunting the woods often find their assignments through him. Stationed within the beleaguered settlement of Darkshire, Calor works closely with Jonathan Carevin, one of the town's prominent defenders. Amid the endless gloom and creeping terrors of Duskwood, Calor devotes himself to supporting the Carevin household's mission: eradicating the supernatural horrors encroaching upon the town's perimeter before the remaining townsfolk are slaughtered. Calor's primary duty centers on confronting the feral worgen prowling the woods, particularly around blighted areas like the Rotting Orchard. These ferocious wolf-men are far more aggressive and dangerous than common beasts, slaughtering travelers and patrols alike. Calor enlists capable fighters to cull their ranks and investigate their origins, desperate to stem the bloodthirsty tide.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_calor-78c3c685807bf80866f4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.7167347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:captain-grayson"] = {
  hash = "becc07",
  fullHash = "195406",
  text = "Captain Grayson. Captain Grayson is the restless spirit of a pirate captain stationed at the Westfall Lighthouse. Having lost his own life when his ship smashed against the dark coastline, he now watches over the beacon to prevent other sailors from suffering his fate. In life, Grayson captained a vessel along the perilous southern seas of the Eastern Kingdoms. His voyage came to a violent end off the jagged shoals of Westfall on a night when the lighthouse fire had gone dark. Cast upon the rocks, Grayson perished alongside his ship, only to awaken as an earthbound phantom trapped upon the coast. Drawn to the Westfall Lighthouse in death, Grayson hoped to find answers about the afterlife. Instead, he witnessed sheer tragedy. A notorious murloc known as Old Murk-Eye drove the lighthouse keeper and his family away. Though the keepers bravely returned to rekindle the flame, Old Murk-Eye rallied the surrounding murlocs for a second, brutal slaughter. Grayson could only watch helplessly as the family was butchered. With the keepers dead and Westfall crumbling under neglect, Captain Grayson took the solemn burden of the beacon upon his spectral shoulders. He seeks aid from passing adventurers to gather fuel for the great flame and avenge the slaughtered family by hunting down Old Murk-Eye and driving back the coastal murloc tide.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_captain-grayson-d81d52c160dc9e4b079d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.9681633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:captain-stoutfist"] = {
  hash = "41d02e",
  fullHash = "6e313c",
  text = "Captain Stoutfist. Captain Stoutfist is the dwarven officer in charge of military defenses at Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands. With the Dragonmaw clan pushing aggressively out of their marshland strongholds, Stoutfist coordinates defensive maneuvers and counter-offensives to ensure the vital Alliance port does not fall. Menethil Harbor serves as the principal deepwater port connecting Ironforge and Stormwind to the marshy lowlands of the Wetlands and the sea beyond. As the town's commanding officer, Captain Stoutfist bears the heavy burden of keeping its docks, civilian populace, and garrison secure against surrounding threats. The marshlands are notoriously hazardous, but the biggest peril to the settlement is an organized surge from renegade orcs. The Dragonmaw clan, entrenched within the Wetlands since the Second War, has begun pushing their operations directly toward the roadways and passes. By infiltrating Dun Algaz, they threaten to sever the overland lifeline connecting Loch Modan and Ironforge to Menethil Harbor. Facing a severe shortage of troops, Captain Stoutfist relies on visiting Alliance volunteers recommended by Mountaineer Valstag Ironjaw to stem the tide.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_captain-stoutfist-d3ec8bc7cc615e8c5bb9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.0718367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:clerk-daltry"] = {
  hash = "9c0766",
  fullHash = "765ff0",
  text = "Clerk Daltry. Clerk Daltry is the civic record keeper stationed in Darkshire Town Hall in Duskwood. Surrounded by ledgers and registry books, he helps investigate the village's past and track down historical records, even if his archives are somewhat neglected and out of date. While Darkshire struggles against encroaching beasts, undead horrors, and supernatural gloom, everyday civic duties fall to Clerk Daltry. Working out of the Darkshire Town Hall alongside Lord Ello Ebonlocke and the local council, Daltry maintains the town's historical ledgers, property rolls, and census data. In a region fractured by disaster, his records are among the few surviving links to the days before Grand Hamlet fell into shadow. Because Darkshire has been cut off from regular Stormwind administration, Daltry's record-keeping is notoriously imperfect. Old documents go missing, ledgers fall behind, and vital town files are frequently misplaced or damaged. Adventurers investigating local cold cases or long-lost residents frequently consult him, only to find that uncovering the truth requires digging through overlooked notes or venturing out into the dangerous woods to recover lost papers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_clerk-daltry-ba0b8b28ec1952af3853/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.96, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:collin-mauren"] = {
  hash = "c0384b",
  fullHash = "0793aa",
  text = "Collin Mauren. Collin Mauren is a human mage residing in the Mage Quarter of Stormwind City. A scholarly researcher and crafter of incantations, he experiments with exotic natural reagents to devise unique utility spells. Among his pursuits is assisting his acquaintance, the gnome engineer and explorer Gaxim Rustfizzle, with specialized arcane scrolls. Situated within Stormwind's bustling Mage Quarter, Collin Mauren spends his days pursuing the quieter, more practical applications of magic. Rather than chasing raw elemental power, he focuses on crafting arcane formulas that solve specific, logistical problems. Collin often turns to natural anomalies and raw components scavenged across Azeroth, weaving their intrinsic properties into functional magic scrolls and reagents for friends and travelers alike. Collin maintains a close correspondence and friendship with the gnomish tinker Gaxim Rustfizzle. When Gaxim needed a reliable way to communicate across great distances during his travels, Collin designed a specialized messaging spell. To anchor the spell, he utilizes the whirling debris left behind by Westfall's erratic Dust Devils, capturing the essence of shifting wind to ensure long-distance messages reach his adventurous colleague. Beyond his work with local elementals, Collin's research extends clear across the Great Sea to the crags of Kalimdor. Curiously captivated by the resilient hides of basilisks native to the Stonetalon Mountains, Collin seeks out their scales to synthesize new spells. Travelers venturing into Kalimdor frequently receive his requests for these rare hides, fueling Collin's ongoing fascination with how primal creature anatomy can stabilize complex enchantments.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_collin-mauren-77f171e4fb1733b55102/1-0000.mp3", duration = 105.247347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:corithras-moonrage"] = {
  hash = "5ccf39",
  fullHash = "acaf12",
  text = "Corithras Moonrage. Corithras Moonrage is a night elf scholar and quest giver stationed in the village of Dolanaar on Teldrassil. He guides newly arrived adventurers in understanding the nature of the great World Tree and tasks them with maintaining its sacred moonwells. Through his lessons, he reveals both the spiritual importance of Teldrassil and the growing burdens carried by the kaldorei. Residing in the shaded settlement of Dolanaar, Corithras Moonrage serves as an instructor to travelers leaving the sheltered bounds of Shadowglen. As young kaldorei venture into the wider boughs of Teldrassil, Corithras ensures they understand the history of their new home. He teaches that survival in this changing era requires vigilance and reverence for the natural order. Much of Corithras's duty revolves around collecting moonwell water for the druids in Darnassus. He directs adventurers to collect pure waters from moonwells across Teldrassil in specially crafted phials for delivery to Fandral Staghelm. Through these trials, he monitors the health of the World Tree and seeks to maintain the delicate balance of the wilderness against creeping corruption.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_corithras-moonrage-28a3b0c498b14216c7ad/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.7616327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:daphne-stilwell"] = {
  hash = "673add",
  fullHash = "6bbeff",
  text = "Daphne Stilwell. Daphne Stilwell tends an isolated orchard nestled atop the coastal cliffs of Westfall overlooking the Longshore. Together with her husband Jordan, she fled turmoil in the north and relocated south, quietly aiding the Church of the Holy Light while defending her home from nearby outlaws. Perched on the bluffs to the west of Moonbrook and the exit of the Deadmines, Daphne Stilwell manages a secluded homestead and apple orchard. From below along the Longshore or above on gryphon routes south to Stranglethorn Vale, the small farm looks quiet and unassuming. Yet running a homestead in modern Westfall is anything but safe. Despite the encroaching bandits and roaming scavengers, Daphne diligently works her trees and keeps watch over her isolated patch of land. Before settling in the hills of Westfall, Daphne and her husband Jordan lived in Lordaeron near Pyrewood Village, working in what is now Shadowfang Keep. When darkness and disaster overtook the region, they were driven southward along with thousands of other human refugees. In Stormwind's orbit, Jordan's renowned craftsmanship and Daphne's steady presence led them to forge close ties with the Church of the Holy Light, providing support and holy arms to paladins of the Silver Hand.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_daphne-stilwell-da9288f8114d371d486d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.8334694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:delgren-the-purifier"] = {
  hash = "a4139c",
  fullHash = "a77b13",
  text = "Delgren the Purifier. Delgren the Purifier is a human paladin stationed at Maestra's Post on the northern edge of Ashenvale. Operating alongside comrades Balthule Shadowstrike and Feero Ironhand, he aids the night elves in cleansing demonic and shadow magic threatening the frontier, with a particular focus on the sinister Cult of the Dark Strand. Human paladins are a rare sight deep within the ancient forests of Kalimdor, but Delgren the Purifier arrived with a mission. Affiliated with the Argent Dawn, Delgren traveled west with a cadre of holy warriors determined to assist the Alliance's new night elf allies against the lingering evils of demons and undead remnants from the Third War. Stationed near the border of Darkshore and Ashenvale, he maintains a watchful vigil over travelers passing south from Auberdine. Despite his fierce devotion to the Holy Light and zealous nickname, Delgren possesses an unusual perspective on Kalimdor's tangled politics. Much to the annoyance and disdain of his conservative superiors, he took the time to learn Orcish. This rare linguistic skill hints at an unconventional mind—one that seeks to understand the enemies and neighbors sharing this untamed continent rather than relying solely on blind dogma.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_delgren-the-purifier-f60227ef85485f8c0339/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.2065306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:denalan"] = {
  hash = "f98110",
  fullHash = "933673",
  text = "Denalan. Denalan is an accomplished night elf herbalist and botanist who tends a quiet lakeside plot along the eastern shore of Lake Al'Ameth. Working under a simple awning, he devotes his days to studying the island's plant life and seeking botanical remedies for the strange corruption affecting Teldrassil. He frequently enlists young adventurers to gather samples, study violent timberlings, and test enchanted soils. While many kaldorei focus on martial vigilance or high druidic rites, Denalan pursues the patient, meticulous craft of herbalism. Stationed along the tranquil eastern banks of Lake Al'Ameth, he has established a modest research camp consisting of a cloth awning, simple field tools, and tidy rows of cultivated planter boxes. Rather than retreating to the grand barrows of Darnassus, he prefers to remain close to the loam, directly examining the unusual flora that has sprouted since the planting of the new World Tree. Denalan's research is far more than an academic curiosity; it addresses the growing unrest among Teldrassil's native wildlife. The timberlings, normally docile woodland beings of wood and sprout, have turned increasingly aggressive. Denalan enchants specialized soil to nurse wild seeds in captivity and analyze how malignant forces seep into the root systems. By studying strange specimens like shimmering fronds, glowing fruit, and moss-twined growths, he seeks to isolate whatever dark taint is troubling the forest boughs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_denalan-b6fdbb0d3e6396c2881a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.9657143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:dirania-silvershine"] = {
  hash = "58d518",
  fullHash = "c66dee",
  text = "Dirania Silvershine. Dirania Silvershine is a night elf resident of Shadowglen in Teldrassil. Deeply loyal and observant, she watches over the tranquil grove and looks out for her companion Iverron when his reckless ventures land him in peril. Dirania stands among the tranquil boughs of Shadowglen, the secluded vale where young night elves begin their service to Kalimdor and the Alliance. While many in the barrow mounds and sanctuaries look to broad spiritual duties, Dirania stays mindful of those nearest to her, attentive to the quiet dangers that stir even in the heart of Teldrassil. Dirania's immediate concern is her companion Iverron. Overeager and careless around the poisonous woodland creatures creeping across the glen, Iverron frequently gets into trouble. When he succumbs to a venomous bite from the local webwood spiders, Dirania takes it upon herself to seek out willing hands to gather ingredients and prepare a remedy before the venom takes a permanent toll.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_dirania-silvershine-3df9a5fc885a8b32063f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.5853061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:dorn-plainstalker"] = {
  hash = "b82730",
  fullHash = "c0a981",
  text = "Dorn Plainstalker. Dorn Plainstalker is a solitary tauren elder residing in the Weathered Nook overlooking the canyons of Thousand Needles. He serves as an overseer of traditional rite of passage trials, challenging Horde adventurers to prove their character and fortitude. Through trials of faith, endurance, strength, and lore, he tests whether travelers are worthy servants of the Earth Mother and their people. Secluded in a shallow cave high atop the canyon ridges of the Weathered Nook, Dorn Plainstalker lives apart from the bustle of Freewind Post. Reaching his mountain redoubt requires winding along the sheer northern cliffs overlooking the towering mesas below. From this quiet vantage point, Dorn maintains ancient customs and judges those who seek guidance in the harsh southern barrens. Dorn Plainstalker guides pilgrims through a demanding series of tests meant to measure the core virtues of a tauren champion: faith, endurance, strength, and lore. Rather than mere exercises in brute force, these trials force aspirants to confront fear, leap blindly into the unknown, outlast cunning wilderness predators, and ponder the sacred history of Kalimdor before claiming their final passage.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_dorn-plainstalker-3b0fe42baba0c74449ce/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.9036735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:duthorian-rall"] = {
  hash = "3eadf2",
  fullHash = "41fa8e",
  text = "Duthorian Rall. Duthorian Rall is a respected human knight of the Silver Hand stationed within Stormwind's Cathedral of Light. Renowned as one of the most influential paladins in the Alliance, he oversees the training and spiritual trials of emerging holy warriors. He guides recruits through solemn vows of charity, valor, and devotion to defend Azeroth against the Scourge and lurking domestic threats. Standing within the vaulted transept of the Cathedral of Light, Duthorian Rall represents the venerable traditions of the Knights of the Silver Hand. With the kingdom unsettled by internal strife and the missing King Varian Wrynn, Duthorian shoulders the vital duty of instilling righteous discipline in fresh initiates. Respected paladins in the field, including Brother Wilhelm in Northshire and Brandur Ironhammer in Ironforge, direct promising recruits to his guidance to begin their sacred trials. Duthorian teaches that martial power without moral foundation is hollow. Through the Tome of Divinity, he tests a paladin's capacity for empathy, requiring acts of mercy and selfless service across Stormwind before entrusting them with deeper mysteries. His trials culminate in testing candidates with the Symbol of Life near Heroes' Vigil, sending them to aid wounded allies and teach them to channel the Light's miraculous power to restore fallen comrades from death.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_duthorian-rall-ef19d88051f08271d7e2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.2522449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:elling-trias"] = {
  hash = "ecbf2b",
  fullHash = "a4df76",
  text = "Elling Trias. Elling Trias manages Trias' Cheese, a small storefront nestled in the bustling Trade District of Stormwind City. While he presents himself as a jovial and passionate purveyor of dairy, he quietly operates as one of the kingdom's most connected information brokers. Behind wheels of cheese and cordial banter, Trias maintains an ear to the ground that reaches across continents. To the casual customer strolling the Trade District, Elling Trias is merely an artisan dedicated to fine curds, working his shop alongside his wife Elaine and son Ben. He enthusiastically hawks wheels of cheese and chats amicably with townsfolk. Yet those in the know understand that Trias' Cheese is one of the most reliable fronts in Stormwind. The steady hum of city commerce provides perfect cover for operatives, courtiers, and adventurers who need to trade quiet words without drawing the attention of the city watch. Elling's network of informants and allies is staggering for a shopkeeper. He maintains ties with Master Mathias Shaw of SI:7, coordinates with Church contacts like Bishop DeLavey, and corresponds with agents as far afield as Darkshire, Theramore Isle, and the distant Plaguelands near Hearthglen. When odd rumors surface—whether concerning highway bandits or quiet movements in the countryside—Trias often pieces the puzzle together long before the royal court takes notice.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_elling-trias-c0d04a77a1690beb4db0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.8269388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:fandral-staghelm"] = {
  hash = "283e85",
  fullHash = "b58c77",
  text = "Fandral Staghelm. Archdruid Fandral Staghelm leads the druids of the Cenarion Circle from the Cenarion Enclave in Darnassus. Stepping into power after Malfurion Stormrage vanished into the Emerald Dream, he championed the planting of the new World Tree, Teldrassil. Proud, abrasive, and relentlessly ambitious, Fandral advocates for an aggressive Kaldorei resurgence that frequently pits him against High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind. Following the Third War and the sacrifice of Nordrassil atop Mount Hyjal, the night elves lost their immortality and their sacred sanctuary. While Malfurion Stormrage cautioned patience and warned against vanity, Fandral championed the bold creation of a new World Tree off the coast of Kalimdor. When Malfurion subsequently fell comatose within the Emerald Dream, Fandral seized the initiative and led the druids in raising Teldrassil. He envisioned the colossal bough as a monument to Kaldorei endurance and a vessel to restore their lost supremacy, even though the Dragon Aspects pointedly withheld their blessings from the unapproved endeavor. A millennium before the First War, Fandral served as the Kaldorei field commander in Silithus during the War of the Shifting Sands. Renowned among the insectoid qiraji as 'Kar'sis'—the Hand of Earth—he held the front lines alongside his beloved only son, Valstann. Exploiting Fandral's devotion, the qiraji General Rajaxx engineered a feint against Southwind Village and captured Valstann, ruthlessly executing him directly in front of the horrified Archdruid. The loss shattered Fandral's spirit, precipitating a devastating retreat and cementing an abiding, bitter hatred for the swarm that remains etched into his haughty demeanor. Within the marble boughs of Darnassus, Fandral governs Kaldorei affairs with a haughty, unyielding hand. Unlike High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, who favors spiritual preservation and cautious diplomacy, Fandral views military expansion and decisive strength as paramount to the survival of their people.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_fandral-staghelm-a90dca3a2c6a17c87cbc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 125.466122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:farthing"] = {
  hash = "014af8",
  fullHash = "c7fe2e",
  text = "Farthing. Bishop Farthing is a human clergyman residing within the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind City. To visitors and worshippers, he presents himself as a devout servant of the Holy Light who guides parishioners and directs aid toward beleaguered settlements across the kingdom. Within the serene marble halls of the Cathedral of Light, Bishop Farthing stands as one of the prominent figures attending to the spiritual needs of Stormwind City. Alongside Archbishop Benedictus and the gathered clergy, he offers counsel to travelers, assists those pursuing the holy arts, and attends to the civic and pastoral obligations of the Church of the Holy Light. Despite remaining situated within the capital, Farthing directs earnest travelers toward darker corners of the kingdom that desperately require holy aid. When troubled reports filter in regarding the necromancer Morbent Fel and the creeping undead blight overtaking Duskwood, Farthing serves as an advisor in seeking out methods to overcome dark sorceries and cleanse the blighted frontier.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_farthing-4634edf177528523e3f9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.2122449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:first-mate-fitzsimmons"] = {
  hash = "5ea774",
  fullHash = "4b4d2d",
  text = "First Mate Fitzsimmons. First Mate Fitzsimmons is a ragged, reeking sailor who spends his days drinking outside the Deepwater Tavern in Menethil Harbor. Once the first mate aboard the Wave Mistress of the Kul Tiras Third Fleet, he is the sole known living survivor of the fleet's destruction during the Second War. Haunted by the tragedy, he seeks willing adventurers to face the restless spirits of his drowned comrades. Lounging outside the Deepwater Tavern amid the damp salt air of Menethil Harbor, First Mate Fitzsimmons is an easy man to overlook—or avoid entirely, given his unwashed stench. To most patrons, he is just another broken old salt drinking his memories away. Yet those willing to sit and listen will hear harrowing tales of a proud fleet brought low by fire and claw. During the Second War, Fitzsimmons served as first mate aboard the Wave Mistress, part of the Kul Tiras Third Fleet dispatched to fight the Horde. Off the coast of the Wetlands, the fleet was ambushed and incinerated by enslaved red dragons flown by Dragonmaw orcs. The ships were shattered and sunk, leaving Fitzsimmons as the only man to drag himself ashore alive.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_first-mate-fitzsimmons-a4fba3949b27ef9ab1e0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.3885714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:foreman-oslow"] = {
  hash = "82e2b8",
  fullHash = "757d90",
  text = "Foreman Oslow. Foreman Oslow is Lakeshire's beleaguered master builder, tasked with repairing the ruined Everstill Bridge in the heart of Redridge Mountains. Beset by Blackrock orc artillery and gnoll raiders, his reconstruction efforts have stalled due to lost shipments and sunken equipment. He relies heavily on traveling adventurers to retrieve his gear and secure materials so Lakeshire can reconnect its vital trade route. Standing by the fractured masonry of the Everstill Bridge, Foreman Oslow faces an uphill battle. The bridge is the lifeline of Lakeshire, spanning the expanse of Lake Everstill and linking the western roads toward Elwynn to the eastern shores. Blackrock orcs, sweeping down from the crags of Stonewatch, targeted the crossing to cripple the town's logistics and military movement, leaving the settlement splintered and vulnerable. Every attempt Oslow makes to rebuild seems plagued by misfortune. When the bridge fell, his replacement tools were shipped overland from Goldshire and loaded onto a lake ferry. Orc catapult crews caught the vessel in their sights, sending his heavy iron toolbox straight to the muddy bed of Lake Everstill. To compound matters, local Redridge gnolls ambushed his supply wagons, making off with timber and mortar rivets necessary to raise the arches.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_foreman-oslow-09ef530052bdbd538c02/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.8783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:furen-longbeard"] = {
  hash = "c59646",
  fullHash = "775128",
  text = "Furen Longbeard. Furen Longbeard is a master dwarven armorer stationed within Stormwind City's bustling Dwarven District. Apprenticed under the renowned smith Grimand Elmore, he specializes in crafting heavy protective gear and sturdy mail for seasoned defenders of the Alliance. Warriors across the kingdom seek out his metallurgical expertise to forge specialized, battle-ready armor. Furen Longbeard plies his craft far from the great forges of Ironforge, making his home instead among the cobblestones and soot of Stormwind's Dwarven District. Like many of his kin, Furen brought traditional dwarven metallurgy south to support the rebuilding and defense of the kingdom. Working amidst blazing hearths and clanging anvils, he maintains strong ties with other artisans throughout the city and beyond. Taught by the venerable craftsman Grimand Elmore, Furen has perfected techniques for treating heavy mail and plate to withstand extreme battlefield punishment. Promising young warriors seeking durable protection often turn to Furen, who requires rare ores, specialized components, and exotic reagents from distant corners of the world to fashion his signature fire-hardened armor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_furen-longbeard-a6245204d2fd621a7b6a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.9485714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gakin-the-darkbinder"] = {
  hash = "1d2b80",
  fullHash = "8c5eaa",
  text = "Gakin the Darkbinder. Gakin the Darkbinder is a master warlock operating covertly deep beneath The Slaughtered Lamb tavern in Stormwind's Mage Quarter. He instructs aspiring Alliance practitioners in the dark arts, testing their resolve before initiating rituals to summon and bind otherworldly demons to their service. While Stormwind's mages practice openly in their towering sanctuaries, practitioners of shadow and fel must dwell where prying eyes cannot reach. Hidden below the taproom of The Slaughtered Lamb is a damp crypt-like cellar equipped with summoning circles and grim tomes. Gakin serves as the anchor of this hidden conclave, ensuring that young warlocks learn discipline and restraint before unleashing horrors they cannot control. Gakin's expertise lies in demonology and binding rituals. Initiates who earn his notice are sent across the kingdom to acquire rare relics and ingredients—from stolen bloodstone talismans to corrupt wood from the dark forests. Once the reagents are assembled, Gakin oversees rituals in the crypt's summoning circle, forcing novitiates to subjugate voidwalkers and succubi in life-or-death trials of willpower.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gakin-the-darkbinder-e119d47b3f5fc54a196d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.7134694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gaxim-rustfizzle"] = {
  hash = "5dcb1f",
  fullHash = "248455",
  text = "Gaxim Rustfizzle. Gaxim Rustfizzle is an ambitious gnome scholar and tinker stationed at a small Alliance camp along the Webwinder Path in Stonetalon Mountains. A representative of the Enlightened Assembly of Arcanology, Alchemy and Engineering Sciences, he seeks to prove to skeptical night elves that advanced artifice and alchemy can preserve their woodland realm. His scientific curiosity is matched by a fierce vendetta against the destructive operations of the Venture Company. While night elves traditionally rely on ancient druidism and nature magic, Gaxim Rustfizzle arrived in the craggy passes of Stonetalon to demonstrate a radically different perspective. Representing the Enlightened Assembly of Arcanology, Alchemy and Engineering Sciences, he genuinely believes that calculated mechanisms and chemical solutions can defend and restore Kalimdor's wilderness just as effectively as prayers to Elune. Camped alongside the sentinel Kaela Shadowspear on the Webwinder Path, he works tirelessly to convince his tall, reclusive allies of engineering's ecological merits. Much of Gaxim's energy is channeled against the Venture Company, whose clear-cutting and industrial greed desecrate nearby Windshear Crag. Believing that crude goblin machinery deserves neither respect nor mercy, Gaxim coordinates covert operations and volatile chemical sabotage against their logging operations. To him, crippling their shredders and stealing their schematics is not merely tactical warfare—it is a practical demonstration that superior gnomish intellect always triumphs over careless corporate plunder.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gaxim-rustfizzle-003d9b225a0a016c11dd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 92.4473469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:grelin-whitebeard"] = {
  hash = "a10bb3",
  fullHash = "43e614",
  text = "Grelin Whitebeard. Grelin Whitebeard is a dwarven scout stationed in Coldridge Valley by royal decree to assess the Frostmane troll threat. Operating out of an isolated camp with a bumbling apprentice, he relies on rising adventurers to keep the trolls contained while Ironforge's attention remains consumed by the trogg crisis. Grelin Whitebeard and his brother Senir were dispatched to Coldridge Valley under direct orders from the Senate of Ironforge and King Magni Bronzebeard himself. Their mission was clear: assess and neutralize the threat posed by the native Frostmane trolls. With Ironforge's official military fully bogged down combating trogg outbreaks across Dun Morogh, the brothers were given very few resources to accomplish their task. Grelin had to establish an outpost in the snowy hills and look toward local recruits and passing initiates to carry out the necessary work. From his camp, Grelin monitors the encroaching Frostmane tribe south of Coldridge Valley. The trolls have grown increasingly aggressive, staging bolder incursions outside their mountain caverns. During one such raid, a heavily painted brute named Grik'nir the Cold slipped into the unattended camp and made off with Grelin's field journal, containing his critical observations. Unwilling to fail the King, Grelin uses intrepid travelers to strike deep into the Frostmane caves, recover his stolen writings, and ensure his final intelligence reaches Senir in Kharanos.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_grelin-whitebeard-7d0ddae0b901e68e6b51/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.1404082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gryan-stoutmantle"] = {
  hash = "ed4bcb",
  fullHash = "1fc6ab",
  text = "Gryan Stoutmantle. Gryan Stoutmantle is a paladin of the Silver Hand and the commander of the People's Militia, operating out of Sentinel Hill in Westfall. Born in Westfall, he returned from the devastated northern kingdom of Lordaeron to protect his homeland after Stormwind's crown abandoned the region to banditry. Gryan Stoutmantle began his service far from the quiet fields of southern Azeroth. Traveling north to Lordaeron as a young man, he took vows as a Knight of the Silver Hand and endured the horrors of the Third War, even witnessing the tragic purge of Stratholme by Prince Arthas Menethil. When news reached him that his native province of Westfall was collapsing into lawlessness, overrun by organized brigands and abandoned by the crown, Gryan left the northern fronts behind. He traded imperial battlefields for the defense of honest farmers, swearing that his home would not fall as the north had. Upon his return, Gryan found Westfall virtually undefended. Due to royal neglect and political paralysis within the House of Nobles, Stormwind refused to dispatch garrison troops. In response, Stoutmantle established the People's Militia at Sentinel Hill, rallying local farmers, sentries, and traveling mercenaries into a volunteer defense corps. His forces hold a precarious line against the Defias Brotherhood, riverpaw gnolls, and hostile mechanoids, unable even to spare reinforcements when neighbor settlements like Lakeshire plead for aid.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gryan-stoutmantle-a6d0ecc9de483ec335a5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.8685714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:gwennyth-bly-leggonde"] = {
  hash = "35d7c2",
  fullHash = "b70a77",
  text = "Gwennyth Bly'Leggonde. Gwennyth Bly'Leggonde is a night elf residing in the coastal port of Auberdine. Stationed along the misty Darkshore coast, she watches the tides and directs travelers to investigate the strange remains washing ashore. Her concerns center on the mysterious sea creatures and massive turtles whose carcasses have begun littering the northern beaches. Gwennyth Bly'Leggonde makes her home in Auberdine, the bustling seaside haven of Darkshore. While many in town concern themselves with the forest or shipping across the Veiled Sea, Gwennyth keeps her gaze firmly on the coastline. The tides have recently turned grim, depositing the remains of colossal marine life onto the surf. To understand what is happening in the waters offshore, she relies on willing adventurers to scour the sands, retrieve skeletal samples, and bring back answers. The beaches north and south of Auberdine have seen an alarming influx of beached sea creatures and ancient sea turtles. Rather than letting their deaths go unnoticed, Gwennyth tasks travelers with inspecting these fallen titans. By studying the bones and discovering what drove these beasts ashore or took their lives, she hopes to piece together the hidden perils lurking off the shores of northern Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_gwennyth-bly-leggonde-7d826b5bb3d8475624e0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.24, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:high-executor-darthalia"] = {
  hash = "bbc7c6",
  fullHash = "f7df5d",
  text = "High Executor Darthalia. High Executor Darthalia is a high-ranking Forsaken military commander stationed at Tarren Mill in the Hillsbrad Foothills. Acting under the direct authority of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and Varimathras, she directs Forsaken operations against the human stronghold of Southshore and local Syndicate brigands. Her leadership is central to securing the Horde's southern flank in Lordaeron. Operating out of Tarren Mill, High Executor Darthalia serves as the operational commander for Forsaken forces throughout the Hillsbrad Foothills. Her primary strategic concern is halting the expansion of human resistance, keeping the Alliance forces in Southshore contained, and hunting down the lawless Syndicate rogues infesting the surrounding countryside. Every offensive push into southern Hillsbrad flows directly through her command. Under direct orders originating from the Undercity's high leadership—the Banshee Queen and her dreadlord lieutenant, Varimathras—Darthalia oversees the relentless attrition war across Hillsbrad. She coordinates scouting missions, targeted strikes against human leadership, and bounties on dangerous outlaws who threaten the Forsaken hold on northern Lordaeron.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_high-executor-darthalia-f7d435e21f06b3c0ecd7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.3885714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:high-priestess-laurena"] = {
  hash = "7a8e40",
  fullHash = "09e1fd",
  text = "High Priestess Laurena. High Priestess Laurena is a respected human priest trainer stationed behind the grand altar within Stormwind City's Cathedral of Light. Having escaped the destruction of Lordaeron as a refugee during the Third War, she now instructs budding Alliance priests in spiritual devotion and arcane wards. Before taking her post within the towering halls of the Cathedral of Light, Laurena dwelt in the northern kingdom of Lordaeron. When the Scourge swept across the land during the Third War, she and her entire family were fortunate enough to survive and flee southward, arriving in Stormwind City as displaced refugees. Despite the immense grief and chaos that shattered her homeland, Laurena remained steadfast in her devotion, eventually rising to become a High Priestess in service to the Church of the Holy Light. Positioned directly behind the central altar within the cathedral sanctuary, High Priestess Laurena dedicates herself to mentoring aspiring priests. When promising acolytes demonstrate sufficient dedication and piety, they are directed to her to deepen their studies. Laurena guides them in the invocation of sacred boons such as Desperate Prayer and instructs them in defensive arts, including spells designed to turn enemy arcane magic back against those who wield it. Once an initiate has learned all she can offer, she sends them onward to other master priests across the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_high-priestess-laurena-941a8350d064a858df9f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.3306122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:illiyana"] = {
  hash = "fbcce9",
  fullHash = "d84ad3",
  text = "Illiyana. Illiyana is an ancient dryad stationed in southwestern Ashenvale, dedicated to protecting the woodland from ancient corruption. Wandering near the Shrine of Aessina, she rallies champions of the Alliance to push back against the vicious satyrs that plague the ancient groves. Her vigilance represents the enduring bond between the daughters of Cenarius and the night elven wilderness. Like all dryads, Illiyana is one of the daughters of the demigod Cenarius. Peaceful yet fiercely protective of wild lands, she patrols the woods of southwestern Ashenvale. As dark forces stir and the woodlands suffer under creeping corruption, Illiyana keeps watch over sacred relics and groves, acting as a steward for nature's primal sanctity. Illiyana's primary concern in the region is the lingering blight of the satyrs. These twisted, demonic creatures continuously seek to corrupt the glades and endanger her dryad sisters. Illiyana frequently enlists passing travelers to strike down these vile abominations and recover lost artifacts, including the Branch of Cenarius, to ensure the woods remain sheltered from demonic taint.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_illiyana-462f9678562382bf1547/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.7616327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:jaina-proudmoore"] = {
  hash = "775d87",
  fullHash = "611007",
  text = "Jaina Proudmoore. Lady Jaina Proudmoore is the ruler of Theramore Isle and the most powerful human sorceress alive. Formerly a star pupil of Dalaran's Kirin Tor, she led the survivors of Lordaeron across the Great Sea and now strives to maintain a fragile peace between the Alliance and the New Horde. The daughter of Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore of Kul Tiras, Jaina showed remarkable aptitude for the arcane at an early age and studied under Archmage Antonidas in Dalaran. During the Third War, she investigated the mysterious Plague of Undeath alongside Prince Arthas Menethil, witnessing firsthand the tragic downfall of Lordaeron. Guided by the warnings of the Prophet Medivh, she rallied an expedition of human survivors and set sail westward to the forgotten continent of Kalimdor. There, her forces forged an unprecedented alliance with Thrall's Horde and the night elves, standing together atop Mount Hyjal to defeat Archimonde and shatter the Burning Legion's invasion. Following the Legion's defeat, Jaina founded the island citadel of Theramore off the coast of Dustwallow Marsh, establishing the primary bastion of the Alliance in southern Kalimdor. Her commitment to diplomacy was tested to its limits when her father arrived seeking vengeance against the orcs; Jaina refused to support his war, stepping aside as Thrall's forces slew the Grand Admiral to prevent Kalimdor from plunging into another devastating conflict. Today, she rules Theramore as a steadfast advocate for coexistence, working closely with Warchief Thrall while anchoring the Alliance's presence against rising local tensions.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_jaina-proudmoore-d3b3fd30f60fb2f641e7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.8171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:jern-hornhelm"] = {
  hash = "cefa88",
  fullHash = "a79fb8",
  text = "Jern Hornhelm. Jern Hornhelm is a dwarven miner affiliated with the Explorers' League, stationed in the town of Thelsamar. He coordinates vital logistics and communication between the settlement, Ironforge, and the archaeological dig site deeper in Loch Modan. Adventurers frequently assist him with shuttling mining supplies and progress reports across the region. Stationed within the rustic town of Thelsamar, Jern Hornhelm plays a key logistical role for dwarven operations throughout Loch Modan. While dwarven scholars and field teams venture out into the wilderness to unearth their ancestral past, maintaining consistent supply chains remains essential. Jern ensures that vital resources, tools, and communications reach the active dig sites safely. As a member of the Explorers' League, Jern bridges the gap between field archaeologists and the League's leadership in the Hall of Explorers in Ironforge. Sites such as Ironband's Excavation rely heavily on blasting powder, equipment, and regular dispatches to keep work moving forward. Jern enlists reliable travelers to navigate the trogg-infested roads and keep the dig fully operational.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_jern-hornhelm-53f3b05f3c18808a0ce9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.3436735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:jitters"] = {
  hash = "5918c8",
  fullHash = "6e1802",
  text = "Jitters. Jitters is a terrified, half-starved human hiding among the ruins of Raven Hill in Duskwood. He clings to scraps of food and struggles to speak about the horrors he has witnessed. Found shivering amid the dilapidated houses of Raven Hill, Jitters lives in constant terror of the predators stalking Duskwood's western trails. He relies on passing adventurers for simple sustenance, desperate for food while keeping a frantic eye on the encroaching shadows. His skittish mannerisms and uncanny reflex for leaping clear of sudden danger earned him his moniker long ago, but his frantic paranoia is born from firsthand encounters with horrific horrors.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_jitters-e7c6c51a09ff4d541fec/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.164898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:jordan-stilwell"] = {
  hash = "d0b3a0",
  fullHash = "f284ef",
  text = "Jordan Stilwell. Jordan Stilwell is a skilled human weaponsmith stationed outside the snowy Gates of Ironforge in Dun Morogh. Though originally meant to become a paladin of the Holy Light, he followed his father's craft to become an artisan whose work serves paladins across the Alliance. He is best known for forging Verigan's Fist in honor of his old friend, Bosch Verigan. Though Jordan Stilwell was expected to take holy vows and become a paladin within the Church of the Holy Light, he ultimately chose the way of the forge. He followed in his father's footsteps, taking up his father's enchanted smithing hammer. Jordan refined his craft under the tutelage of the dwarven blacksmith Bailor Stonehand. Despite setting aside the mantle of an anointed holy warrior, Jordan remains deeply faithful to the Light, dedicating his extraordinary craftsmanship to outfitting champions who fight in its name. Long before setting up shop in the biting cold of Dun Morogh, Jordan served as a smith at the keep of Baron Silverlaine in Silverpine Forest. When Archmage Arugal unleashed the feral worgen upon the fortress, turning it into the cursed bastion known as Shadowfang Keep, Jordan managed to escape the bloodshed. Forced to abandon his forge and his former home, he eventually found refuge among the dwarves of Dun Morogh.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_jordan-stilwell-75309d983d923b50cc4c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.8636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:kaela-shadowspear"] = {
  hash = "3bcc5f",
  fullHash = "384b8c",
  text = "Kaela Shadowspear. Kaela Shadowspear is a night elf sentinel stationed southwest of Windshear Crag in the Stonetalon Mountains. From her small outpost, she coordinates with fellow sentinels to monitor escalating tensions and regional disruptions in the wilderness. She relies on brave travelers to scout dangerous territory and bring aid to the land's wounded defenders. Positioned just southwest of the industrial devastation of Windshear Crag, Kaela Shadowspear keeps a watchful vigil over the surrounding passes. The Stonetalon Mountains are fraught with danger as the Horde encroaches and goblin operations exploit the land's natural bounty. Operating alongside the gnome Gaxim Rustfizzle, Kaela acts as an essential forward liaison for the night elves, tracking threats that push deeper into ancestral territory. The unrest in Stonetalon goes far beyond territorial skirmishes. The violent disruption of nature has agitated the elementals and brought harm to ancient guardians of the forest. Kaela works tirelessly to dispatch adventurers against raging elemental spirits and to tend to the wounded ancients suffering in the crossfire of the region's conflicts, striving to restore peace before the land is irrevocably scarred.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_kaela-shadowspear-9f8e5db1eeaf084f91c9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.9118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:rethiel-the-greenwarden"] = {
  hash = "e402f1",
  fullHash = "b32ba5",
  text = "Rethiel the Greenwarden. Rethiel the Greenwarden is an ancient, sentient bog beast residing in the Green Belt within the Wetlands. Deeply attuned to the natural balance of the marsh, he watches over the land and enlists travelers to cull threats that disrupt its harmony. Standing amidst the damp thickets of the Green Belt, Rethiel is a towering creature of moss, peat, and primal plant life. While many bog beasts in the marshlands wander aimlessly as aggressive predators, Rethiel possesses a rare, conscious awareness and a sorrowful voice. He perceives the wetlands not merely as murky terrain, but as a living ecosystem that currently cries out under the weight of foreign predation and corruption. Rethiel seeks to alleviate the suffering of the Wetlands by dispatching sympathetic adventurers against its major agitators. He urges visitors to hunt down destructive Mosshide gnolls who ravage the wildlife, strip them of the crude flints they use to burn the marsh's living trees, and thin out the diseased fen creepers that fester in the marsh waters. Through these cullings, he hopes to restore peace to the wounded soil.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_rethiel-the-greenwarden-80f11de1de61e004ad00/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.9706122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["npc:shael-dryn"] = {
  hash = "7fdf7d",
  fullHash = "409904",
  text = "Shael'dryn. Shael'dryn is an inquisitive dryad residing beside the Moonwell of Purity, east of Iris Lake in Ashenvale. She serves as a vital contact for night elf scouts investigating forest corruption. She plays a pivotal role in aiding Astranaar's sentinels with cleansing relics to combat demonic and foul influences spreading across the wilderness. Stationed beside the Moonwell of Purity, Shael'dryn keeps watch alongside the night elf sentinels guarding the eastern approaches of the forest. When corruption surfaces among the local wildlife or tainted relics threaten the land's harmony, her deep natural understanding allows her to guide adventurers through the steps required to restore spiritual balance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_npc_shael-dryn-035290db39335158cb66/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.24, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:alexston-farmstead"] = {
  hash = "32969a",
  fullHash = "8e9538",
  text = "Alexston Farmstead. The Alexston Farmstead is a derelict farm in Westfall, overgrown and prowled by haywire Harvest Watchers. Once the childhood home of Stormwind's city architect, Baros Alexston, it has fallen into ruin like much of the breadbasket around it. Situated amidst the dry plains of Westfall, the Alexston Farmstead stands as another grim testament to the kingdom's neglect. Like neighboring estates, the farm was abandoned by its caretakers when banditry erupted across the countryside. Today, the fields are overgrown, and the mechanical Harvest Watchers left behind to tend the crops have spun out of control, violently attacking anyone who approaches the barren farmhouse. Despite its bleak state, the farm is tied to one of Stormwind's most prominent citizens. Baros Alexston, the skilled architect who helped design parts of the rebuilt capital, spent his youth toiling on these very fields. He left the farmstead long before the unrest began to pursue his trade in masonry and construction, leaving behind a memento of his early life nestled among the ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_alexston-farmstead-278520a11cc9fe3bf746/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.2236735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:alterac-mountains"] = {
  hash = "851809",
  fullHash = "98858d",
  text = "Alterac Mountains. The Alterac Mountains are a rugged, snow-capped range rising directly above the rolling pastures of Hillsbrad. Once the heart of a proud human kingdom, the slopes now harbor ruined settlements, bandit factions, and dangerous wilderness where travelers rarely walk unguarded. Long before the hills fell into chaos, these highlands formed the sovereign nation of Alterac, one of the seven kingdoms born from ancient Arathor. That legacy shattered during the Second War when King Aiden Perenolde struck a clandestine pact with the Old Horde, allowing orcish forces passage through the peaks in exchange for his kingdom's safety. The Alliance of Lordaeron uncovered the betrayal and deposed Perenolde, leaving the region fractured by martial law and territorial disputes. Following the devastation of the Third War, the mountains collapsed into complete lawlessness. The disinherited nobles of the realm banded together to form the Syndicate, operating out of the ruins of Strahnbrad and plotting to reclaim their lost dominion through sabotage and assassination. Between ruthless Crushridge ogres occupying the shattered capital, marauding gnolls, and the shattered magical ruins around Dalaran, the passes of Alterac remain some of the most perilous pathways in Lordaeron.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_alterac-mountains-dda52ee4539ad60d2a44/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.0016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:alther-s-mill"] = {
  hash = "81875d",
  fullHash = "4b9646",
  text = "Alther's Mill. Alther's Mill is a ruined lumber mill tucked into the rugged hills northeast of Lakeshire in the Redridge Mountains. Once a vital source of processed timber for local builders, the abandoned grounds have deteriorated into a haven for feral scavengers and prowling predators. For adventurers traveling east toward Stonewatch, the dilapidated structures serve as a grim landmark marking the boundary of safe frontier territory. Before the region fell prey to invading predators and orc raids, Alther's Mill bustled with timber trade that supplied the construction of Lakeshire and surrounding settlements. Its quiet clearing northeast of Lake Everstill provided ready access to mountain timber stands. As turmoil mounted across the kingdom of Stormwind and the Blackrock clan pushed down from the heights of Stonewatch, local millers abandoned the facility, leaving the dry timber buildings and wheel to decay into rot. In the absence of human workers, the mill became infested with monstrous tarantulas, carrion vultures, and opportunistic raiders. Travelers cutting through the path to the eastern passes must skirt its shadowed perimeter carefully. In recent years, shadowy operatives and thieves have also taken advantage of the abandoned compound, using its discarded storage boxes and hidden corners to hone illicit lockpicking crafts and covert trades far from the eyes of the Lakeshire Town Watch.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_alther-s-mill-e80516203363e64ba50a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.4236735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:anvilmar"] = {
  hash = "f07758",
  fullHash = "7fd063",
  text = "Anvilmar. Anvilmar is a fortified dwarven outpost nestled in the snowy depths of Coldridge Valley in Dun Morogh. Centered around a massive stone forge, it serves as the primary training grounds and rallying point for young Bronzebeard dwarves and displaced gnomish refugees beginning their adventures. Tucked away in the northern crags of Coldridge Valley, Anvilmar stands as an enduring symbol of dwarven perseverance. Dominated by a roaring central forge, this sturdy masonry complex houses class mentors, supply vendors, and veteran mountain mountaineers who drill recruits before sending them out into the wider kingdom. Though sheltered behind towering drifts, the settlement remains on constant guard against the hostile Frostmane trolls and aggressive wild beasts prowling the frozen valley. The settlement likely takes its proud name from High King Modimus Anvilmar, the ancient sovereign who ruled all dwarven clans before the War of the Three Hammers. However, its history has not been without strife. In bygone days, a towering Frostmane troll managed to breach the perimeter and lay waste to the enclave. Today, the garrison remains vigilant, ensuring the forge fires stay lit and no such disaster ever strikes the starting grounds again.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_anvilmar-92daff425cfc874bc02b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.7885714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:astranaar"] = {
  hash = "495cf6",
  fullHash = "0ebad2",
  text = "Astranaar. Astranaar is the primary Night Elf settlement in central Ashenvale, built across a serene lake island straddling the ancient forest road. Serving as the beating heart of Alliance operations in the region, it provides vital refuge to travelers facing encroaching threats from hostile furbolgs, satyrs, and the Horde. Situated atop an island in the middle of a forest lake, Astranaar stands as the largest night elven settlement in Ashenvale. Connected to the mainland by wooden footbridges spanning the waters, the town houses sentinels, priests, and artisans who keep vigil over the woodlands. In addition to offering basic comforts such as an inn, traders, and an expert leatherworker, its elevated perches host a flight master providing hippogryph travel to distant outposts across Kalimdor. Despite its tranquil waters and glowing lanterns, Astranaar exists in a state of perpetual readiness. Local leaders like Raene Wolfrunner coordinate patrols and defensive actions against the corrupted Thistlefur furbolgs encroaching from the north, fearful they may mount a direct assault on the settlement. The town also bears quiet historical sorrow: across the lake in the cemetery lies the resting place of Valstann Staghelm, who fell a thousand years ago during the War of the Shifting Sands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_astranaar-7de421153b9fbed215bb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.5983673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:auberdine"] = {
  hash = "7a49b2",
  fullHash = "0eaa4d",
  text = "Auberdine. Auberdine is a coastal night elven port town on the gloomy shores of Darkshore, serving as the Alliance's primary maritime gateway to northern Kalimdor. Sheltered beneath overcast skies and pine-covered bluffs, it connects the island home of Teldrassil to the Eastern Kingdoms while serving as an administrative haven against the encroaching wilderness. Resting along the shores of the Veiled Sea, Auberdine is the only major settlement to successfully endure in modern Darkshore. Unlike the secluded treetop cities of Teldrassil, Auberdine looks outward, serving as the Alliance's busiest port in the north. Regular ships sail between here and Menethil Harbor in the Eastern Kingdoms, while local ferries run to Rut'theran Village at the boughs of the World Tree. Because of its trade role, Auberdine embraces a more cosmopolitan air than traditional elven enclaves; visitors find lamps illuminating stone-laid streets, bilingual signs in Darnassian and Common, and outland merchants including dwarves and gnomes mingling among the low-slung, arched timber houses. Governed by elder Thundris Windweaver and the Auberdine Village Council, the town coordinates the defence of Darkshore alongside the local Sentinels. Despite its role as a vital trade hub, highborn denizens of Darnassus often look askance at Auberdine, viewing its population of traders, fishermen, and outsiders as misfits. The town endures relentless regional perils: aggressive murlocs raid the fishing boats along the strand, corrupted Blackwood furbolgs press from the south, and foul magic seeping down from Felwood agitates the local wildlife.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_auberdine-b3ae51abda78c34cf38f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 100.780408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:azurelode-mine"] = {
  hash = "98170d",
  fullHash = "615c72",
  text = "Azurelode Mine. Azurelode Mine is an active Alliance iron mine nestled in the southwestern hills of Hillsbrad Foothills. Guarded by Southshore's militia and worked by human laborers, its steady supply of ore fuels the local human war effort against the Forsaken of Tarren Mill. Situated southwest of the open farmland and just west of Southshore, Azurelode Mine has long tapped into the mineral-rich ridges along the coast. Before the Greymane Wall was sealed, these hills were considered part of Gilnean territory. Today, human miners labor deep underground under the watchful eye of Foreman Bonds, extracting iron to forge arms and armor for the Alliance defenders holding the region against the Undercity. Because Azurelode Mine supplies the bulk of the raw iron needed to maintain Southshore's garrisons and town militia, it sits directly in the crosshairs of the Horde. Tarren Mill has targeted its workforce and guards in an effort to shatter the supply lines that sustain Hillsbrad's human strongholds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_azurelode-mine-5e0aa283bd283e1d8bff/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.0220408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ban-ethil-barrow-den"] = {
  hash = "f5f16b",
  fullHash = "55c42e",
  text = "Ban'ethil Barrow Den. Ban'ethil Barrow Den is a sacred subterranean sanctuary hollowed out within the roots of Teldrassil. Originally created as a resting place for the sleeping Druids of the Talon, the cavern complex has recently fallen into chaos. The corrupted furbolgs of the Gnarlpine tribe have overrun its chambers, turning a place of peaceful slumber into a perilous den of madness. Barrow dens serve as sacred resting chambers where night elf druids slumber while projecting their spirits into the Emerald Dream. Ban'ethil Barrow Den was dedicated specifically to sheltering the Druids of the Talon as they communed with nature. Divided into upper and lower subterranean chambers, the den once resonated with tranquil reverie and deep meditative magic. However, the affliction sweeping Teldrassil's wildlife did not spare the Gnarlpine furbolgs, who invaded the complex, turning its hollowed halls into a fortified warren. Driven mad by mysterious corruption taking root across the World Tree, the Gnarlpine tribe drove out or endangered the slumbering druids within Ban'ethil. The furbolgs now fiercely guard the winding tunnels, aggressive toward any night elf who approaches. For the defenders of Teldrassil, reclaiming the barrow den is an urgent priority, not merely to drive back the hostile furbolgs, but to protect the vulnerable druids and sacred relics trapped within its lower depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ban-ethil-barrow-den-6a1f7683ccf295d6d734/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.7673469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:baradin-bay"] = {
  hash = "ab75bd",
  fullHash = "20f623",
  text = "Baradin Bay. Baradin Bay is the expansive body of water lying between the shores of Khaz Modan, the Wetlands, the Arathi Highlands, and southern Lordaeron. Once vigorously patrolled by the powerful navy of Kul Tiras during the Second War, its waters now conceal treacherous reefs, sunken warships, and hostile aquatic predators. Framed by the coasts of Dun Morogh to the south and Lordaeron to the north, Baradin Bay serves as a vital maritime passage connecting the harbors of the Eastern Kingdoms. The bay is anchored by busy ports like Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands and Southshore in the Hillsbrad Foothills. However, navigating these waters is perilous: shallow sandbanks, jagged shoals, and dangerous fauna like murlocs, crocolisks, and deep-sea sharks pose constant threats to sailors and travelers alike. During the Second War, Baradin Bay witnessed heavy naval conflict as the fleets of Kul Tiras clashed with the Horde. Today, grim reminders of those battles litter the coast and the seabed. The shattered hulls of the Kul Tiran Third Fleet—known locally as the Lost Fleet—rest half-submerged along the Wetlands coast, while other vessels have plummeted down to Drowned Reef. Hostile spirits, opportunistic salvagers, and ravenous sea beasts now haunt the waters surrounding these decaying timber ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_baradin-bay-cd52969f5c7d1f9bfeb4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.7632653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bathran-s-haunt"] = {
  hash = "8faa1f",
  fullHash = "edcc48",
  text = "Bathran's Haunt. Bathran's Haunt is an ancient kaldorei ruin nestled in northwestern Ashenvale, east of the Zoram Strand. Recently, Forsaken herbalists and their guardians have overtaken the site to harvest local flora for their alchemical research, steadily withering the surrounding woodland. North of the Ruins of Ordil'Aran, crumbling night elf pillars stand swallowed by thick Ashenvale foliage. What was once a tranquil kaldorei relic has fallen under the shadow of the Forsaken. Skulking among the broken marble, undead herbalists and their enforcers have set up a crude open-air laboratory. Their presence alone blights the ancient soil: grass rots into a sickly brown, vibrant blossoms wilt, and an unnatural decay creeps through the grove as they methodically harvest rare woodland reagents for their experiments. The primary draw of these ruins is Bathran's Hair, a unique remedial herb found taking root only among these particular stones. While night elf and Alliance scouts view the Forsaken presence as a vile desecration of sacred elven soil, the undead work diligently to extract the plant's potent properties. The Horde maintains a vested interest in the compound's botanical yield, though the Forsaken's secretive methods and the resulting corruption of the earth draw suspicion from outsiders and allies alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bathran-s-haunt-6b1c43c460a697a641e3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.7518367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:beggar-s-haunt"] = {
  hash = "534809",
  fullHash = "33b307",
  text = "Beggar's Haunt. Beggar's Haunt is an abandoned Stormwind scout tower perched on a hillside in northeastern Duskwood, near the mountain road to Deadwind Pass. While originally built to keep watch over the kingdom's eastern borders, it has become an outpost for Forsaken infiltrators deep within Alliance territory. Perched above the foggy hills east of Manor Mistmantle, Beggar's Haunt was once an active observation post manned by the Stormwind Guard to secure the eastern approaches to the realm. As Grand Hamlet collapsed into the gloom of modern Duskwood and kingdom patrols withdrew to Darkshire, the stone tower was abandoned to the elements. Today, its commanding vantage point serves a far more sinister purpose: agents of the Forsaken, including members of the Royal Apothecary Society and Deathstalkers, use the ruined stronghold to conduct research and monitor the region under the Alliance's nose. Behind the tower, a steep, twisting path descends to a secluded pool and waterfall known as Knight's Hollow. Here lies an unmarked grave flanked by two ominous hooded stone statues resembling the guardians flanking the Dark Portal. Nearby resting plots, including the burial site of an Unknown Soldier, speak to forgotten clashes fought along this frontier long before the Forsaken moved into the watchtower.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_beggar-s-haunt-cdcab5ca0ee0db897c3f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.2481633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:boulderslide-ravine"] = {
  hash = "3c8a0f",
  fullHash = "a40b00",
  text = "Boulderslide Ravine. Boulderslide Ravine is a rugged, secluded canyon tucked away in the central peaks of the Stonetalon Mountains, reached via Webwinder Path. Largely spared from the direct clashes between the Horde and Alliance, it serves as the rocky approach to Boulderslide Cavern and remains home to wild mountain beasts. Tucked away off Webwinder Path, Boulderslide Ravine is a narrow cut of rock in the Stonetalon heights. While loggers, night elves, and the Horde battle over timber and sacred glades throughout the region, this craggy pass remains relatively undisturbed by military forces. Wild mountain rams and roaming crag beasts like Cliff Stormers make up the local fauna, navigating the treacherous ledges and sheer stone walls. Prospectors also keep an eye on the ravine, as rich mineral veins frequently crop up along the rocky slopes leading deeper toward the cavern network. Opposite the entrance to Boulderslide Cavern lies a small, quiet cave overlooking the ravine. Lit by rough torches and stacked with weathered supply barrels, the shelter contains primitive tribal paintings across its interior walls. The crude artwork bears a striking resemblance to ancient tauren designs, suggesting that native tribes traveled or sought temporary shelter in these crags long before modern conflict swept into the mountains.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_boulderslide-ravine-e62ab6b00254e4ae462d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.1583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-aparaje"] = {
  hash = "a70dcc",
  fullHash = "c41083",
  text = "Camp Aparaje. Camp Aparaje was once a peaceful tauren village nestled in the eastern heights of the Stonetalon Mountains. Today, it lies overrun and occupied by the hostile Grimtotem tribe, who slaughtered the original settlers and took the outpost for themselves. Perched along the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains, Camp Aparaje was established by peaceful tauren looking to live quietly in the highlands. That tranquility was shattered when raiders from the nearby Grimtotem Post, led by Grundig Darkcloud, launched a brutal and unprovoked assault. The villagers were butchered or driven away, and survivors such as Makaba Flathoof were left mourning the destruction of their home while dreading the fate of captive kin. Now completely occupied by Grimtotem warriors, Camp Aparaje serves as a forward base of operations for the aggressive tribe in the region. Unlike typical Grimtotem settlements that fly blue markings, the great totem standing in Aparaje is carved with deep crimson paint.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-aparaje-2ffb5d5bb7a1c62bbaa0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.3502041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:canals-stormwind-city"] = {
  hash = "34363d",
  fullHash = "495e2e",
  text = "Canals (Stormwind City). The Canals are a network of stone-lined waterways weaving through Stormwind City, separating its distinct districts. They serve as quiet thoroughfares for pedestrians and anglers, while quietly harboring hidden hazards and the entrance to the city's prison. The Canals link and define the districts of Stormwind, dividing the bustling markets of the Trade District from the aged alleys of Old Town, the cathedral gardens, and the Dwarven District. Stone bridges, archways, and waterside walkways frame quiet establishments like the Gallina Winery, tailor shops, and quiet residential homes. In addition to serving local merchants and recreational fishermen, the waterways conceal the fortified entrance to the Stockade, where Stormwind's most dangerous criminals and rebels are held beneath street level. The placid surface of the waterways belies a violent past. Following the sack of Stormwind during the First War, the canals were discovered choked with rubble, timber, and the corpses of slaughtered defenders. When the Alliance of Lordaeron liberated the ruins, soldiers undertook the grim labor of clearing the waterways and burying the dead before rebuilding began. Today, while cleaned and restored, the depths retain an air of mystery, fueled by city rumors of a massive albino crocolisk prowling beneath the docks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_canals-stormwind-city-1246b92ae39990a5d48c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.0204082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cathedral-of-light"] = {
  hash = "39f342",
  fullHash = "07d5c6",
  text = "Cathedral of Light. The Cathedral of Light is the spiritual heart of Stormwind City and the primary sanctuary of the Church of the Holy Light. Standing tall over Cathedral Square, it serves as the central bastion for human priesthood and the Knights of the Silver Hand. Rebuilt after its devastation during the First War under the guidance of Archbishop Alonsus Faol and then-Bishop Benedictus, the Cathedral of Light stands as a towering beacon of resilience. Its vast halls and subterranean catacombs sheltered desperate refugees during the darkest hours of Stormwind's reconstruction. With the fall of Lordaeron to the Scourge during the Third War, the seat of the Church shifted south to Stormwind. Today, Archbishop Benedictus shepherds the faithful from this grand hall, providing spiritual guidance to the entire Alliance. Beyond serving as a house of prayer and healing, the cathedral acts as the primary headquarters for the remaining Knights of the Silver Hand in the southern kingdoms. High-ranking figures such as Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker and Duthorian Rall train promising squires through the sacred trials of the Tome of Valor and the Tome of Divinity. Alongside them, High Priestess Laurena guides devout acolytes in the deepest tenets of the Light, preparing holy champions to defend the realm against the rising threat of the Defias Brotherhood and the Scourge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cathedral-of-light-bbec770d146af67524a7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.5208163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cathedral-square"] = {
  hash = "3e5388",
  fullHash = "249281",
  text = "Cathedral Square. Cathedral Square is the spiritual and civic heart of Stormwind City, dominated by the majestic Cathedral of Light. Beyond serving as a place of worship and devotion, the bustling plaza houses municipal institutions like City Hall, the city orphanage, and the regional headquarters of the Argent Dawn. Situated northwest of the Trade District and southeast of the Dwarven District, Cathedral Square serves as the epicenter of faith in the Kingdom of Stormwind. Dominating the skyline is the Cathedral of Light, the preeminent sanctuary for followers of the Holy Light across Azeroth. Priests, paladins, and healers gather here to study, pray, and train. Citizens, pilgrims, and local schoolchildren led by School Mistress Danna regularly mingle in the square for conversation and contemplation. The district is not merely reserved for prayer; it is one of the capital's busiest civic centers. The square houses the Stormwind Orphanage as well as City Hall, anchoring the administrative life of the kingdom. Specialized armorers and weaponsmiths catering to holy crusaders, such as Righteous Plates and Just Maces, line the avenues. It also serves as the base of operations for the Argent Dawn within Stormwind, where recruiters and representatives coordinate relief efforts and rally champions against the encroaching threats of the Scourge in the northern kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cathedral-square-b9825764741cd7877be7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.7820408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cenarion-enclave"] = {
  hash = "72c55e",
  fullHash = "101af2",
  text = "Cenarion Enclave. The Cenarion Enclave is the verdant northwestern district of Darnassus, serving as the central gathering place and seat of power for the Cenarion Circle in the capital. While Archdruid Malfurion Stormrage lies slumbering within the Emerald Dream, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm leads the night elven druids from the great hollows and living boughs of this grove. Occupying a high terrace above the Temple Gardens, the Cenarion Enclave is shaped from ancient oaks and ashes marked with runic bark. Convocations of the Cenarion Circle gather within structures molded directly from living wood and stone. With Malfurion Stormrage absent in the Emerald Dream, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm oversees affairs here, pushing his ambitious vision for Teldrassil despite lingering political friction with High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind. Beyond the druids who meditate among its streams, the enclave also hosts the capital's elven hunters and the stables where skilled riders learn to tame Darnassian nightsabers. Though renowned as a tranquil sanctuary of nature, the enclave harbors more covert activities. Beneath the hollowed roots of the westernmost bough lies a subterranean refuge used by Darnassus's clandestine rogues, who conduct their quiet business shielded by the rustling canopy above.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cenarion-enclave-fe3427ab13b1c9ee200a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.6432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:charred-vale"] = {
  hash = "0b7e2b",
  fullHash = "5957bb",
  text = "Charred Vale. The Charred Vale is a smoldering valley in southwestern Stonetalon Mountains, devastated by the reckless deforestation and machinery of the Venture Company. Once a thriving haven for treants and chimaeras, it is now engulfed in flames, roving elementals, and hostile harpies. Not long ago, this southwest basin was a flourishing alpine wood guarded by majestic treants and inhabited by native chimaeras and harpies. Its ruin began with the arrival of the Venture Company. The greedy goblin outfit clear-cut the forest with explosive machinery, corrupting the guardians and awakening chaotic fire elementals. Though the treants eventually forced the goblins back, the land was left broken, scarred by fire, and tainted by whatever foul devices the retreating loggers deployed. Today, blackened ancients wander blindly through smoke and ash alongside enraged elementals. Bloodfury harpies roost in the skeletal snags, defending what remains of their territory. Despite the hostility of the valley, druids from Sun Rock Retreat refuse to abandon the region, dispatching travelers to sow enchanted seeds and tend the corrupted ancients in a persistent campaign to reclaim the scorched earth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_charred-vale-3ccf1504aed4d0e93a98/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.6318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:clean-zone"] = {
  hash = "eb67fa",
  fullHash = "601284",
  text = "Clean Zone. The Clean Zone is a fortified, decontamination enclave nestled inside the ruined city of Gnomeregan just past the upper Hall of Gears. It serves as the sole safe haven within the irradiated facility where uncorrupted gnomish forces hold ground against surrounding troggs and leper gnomes. Here, field medics, technicians, and specialized Sparklematic machines offer respite and salvage operations amid the radioactive ruins. While most of Gnomeregan succumbed to Mekkatorque's disastrous venting of radioactive waste, the Clean Zone remains untainted. Positioned strategically between the trogg-infested Dormitory below and the contested corridors of the Hall of Gears above, it acts as a fortified forward base. Resolute gnome defenders, technicians, and field medics hold the barricades day and night, ensuring Alliance expeditions have a stable staging ground to regroup, mend wounds, and maintain machinery. Beyond serving as a military redoubt, the Clean Zone is a hub for salvaging the lost capital. Gnomish engineers installed Sparklematic 5200 units to chemically scrub and wash grime-encrusted relics hauled out of the city's radioactive underbelly. Technicians and merchants like Tink Sprocketwhistle process these reclaimed gears and baubles, outfitting adventurers while studying the lingering toxic fallout that plagues the deep tunnels.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_clean-zone-ab03b5f8e4b99b1e1057/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.315102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cleft"] = {
  hash = "1c0824",
  fullHash = "8246d1",
  text = "Cleft. The Cleft is a darkened hollow located northwest of Dolanaar in Teldrassil. Once a tranquil corner of the boughs, it has been claimed by the corrupted Gnarlpine furbolgs, who use it as a stronghold to ambush travelers moving along the main highway toward Darnassus. Situated off the highway between Dolanaar and Darnassus, the Cleft has turned into one of the most perilous dens on the world tree. Driven mad by the foul taint spreading through Teldrassil, the resident Gnarlpine furbolgs have fortified this hollow. From this redoubt, the maddened beasts terrorize the local roads, threatening to cut Dolanaar off from the capital.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cleft-0296d455832ece997f98/1-0000.mp3", duration = 37.9297959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:command-center"] = {
  hash = "eb026d",
  fullHash = "665c4f",
  text = "Command Center. The Command Center, often referred to as the Stormwind Barracks, stands in the heart of Old Town as the operational headquarters for the city's military officers. In peacetime, it houses the standing army, but with Stormwind's forces dispatched to distant frontiers, it currently operates with a seasoned skeleton crew focused on drilling recruits and local militia. Situated within the weathered streets of Old Town, the Command Center serves as the primary garrison and martial training ground for the kingdom of Stormwind. Normally packed with regular infantry and ranking officers, the facility is uncharacteristically sparse. With much of the royal army committed to campaigns abroad, the barracks relies on a veteran cadre of instructors—including Ander Germaine and martial artist Wu Shen—to train fresh warriors, while figures like Wulf Hansreim drill marksmen to bolster the city's defenses. Guards throughout Stormwind often direct newcomers to the 'Barracks' when seeking instruction in heavy arms and tactical combat. The building's proximity to both the Pig and Whistle Tavern and the shadowy operations elsewhere in Old Town makes it an essential anchor of order in a district known for tavern brawls and roguish activity.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_command-center-1e9c169d6f8183835986/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.3779592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:commons"] = {
  hash = "14242a",
  fullHash = "965900",
  text = "Commons. The Commons serves as the vibrant commercial heart of Ironforge, connecting the city entrance directly to its vast caverns. Teeming with merchants, travelers, and bankers, it is the busiest thoroughfare in the dwarven capital and the central marketplace for the entire Alliance. Located just northeast of the Gates of Ironforge, the Commons is the first district that greets travelers entering the mountain kingdom. Carved directly out of the stone, this bustling market street houses the primary Alliance Auction House, the Stonefire Tavern, and the city's central bank within the Vault of Ironforge. Broad avenues connect it seamlessly to the Great Forge, the Mystic Ward, and the Military Ward, ensuring that nearly all foot traffic in the mountain passes through its bustling storefronts. Due to Ironforge's central location on the Eastern Kingdoms continent and its direct underground Deeprun Tram link to Stormwind, the Commons functions as the Alliance's primary trade nexus. Adventurers converge here to trade goods brought from every corner of Azeroth, purchase armaments from merchants like Steelfury and Barim, or meet under the warm lantern light of the local inn before heading out into the Khaz Modan snows.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_commons-d1a392cbc13d2e7da344/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.7918367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:craftsmen-s-terrace"] = {
  hash = "176469",
  fullHash = "e626b9",
  text = "Craftsmen's Terrace. The Craftsmen's Terrace is the bustling artisan district situated in the northern reaches of Darnassus. It serves as the primary trade and manufacturing hub for the night elf capital, housing workshops, profession trainers, and a lively inn. The district also hosts external representatives, including an embassy for the neutral Argent Dawn. Spanning the northern edge of the canopy city, the Craftsmen's Terrace gathers the diverse trades that keep Darnassus running. Woodworkers, leathercrafters, alchemists, and other artisans operate their shops here, integrating classic night elven methods with practical craft. The district also provides essential community amenities, including an inn, a guild master, and guild petition vendors. Bridges connect the terrace directly to the Cenarion Enclave to the west and the Warrior's Terrace to the south. Despite the kaldorei's historic isolation, the Craftsmen's Terrace is home to foreign connections, most notably an embassy established by the Argent Dawn to coordinate with northern allies against the Scourge. Visitors browsing the district's various workshops might also stumble across scattered scholarly volumes, ranging from historical accounts of the War of the Ancients to chronicles detailing the Second War and esoteric treatises on the Old Gods.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_craftsmen-s-terrace-ebe294f952d229b75d0f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.9681633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cragpool-lake"] = {
  hash = "b9f011",
  fullHash = "20297c",
  text = "Cragpool Lake. Cragpool Lake is a body of water tucked inside Windshear Crag in the Stonetalon Mountains. Looming above its dark waters sits a massive timber structure erected by the Venture Company. Nestled within Windshear Crag, Cragpool Lake lies directly beneath the towering logging apparatus built by the Venture Company. An enormous wooden structure spans above the water, serving as the vantage point from which Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle oversees operations across the mountain valley. Venture Company workers occupy the platforms overhead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cragpool-lake-2d44b0771e2d57d4470e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 34.2465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dagger-hills"] = {
  hash = "b320e6",
  fullHash = "d5a779",
  text = "Dagger Hills. The Dagger Hills form a rugged ridge rising across southern Westfall, directly behind the blighted town of Moonbrook. Long neglected by the crown, this rocky highland serves as a hostile hideout for cutthroats, bandits, and creeping terrors that threaten the surrounding plains. Rising high above the dilapidated settlement of Moonbrook, the Dagger Hills mark the jagged southern reaches of Westfall. Stripped of official royal patrols from Stormwind, this arid highland has become a natural fortification for the Defias Brotherhood. Knuckledusters, pathstalkers, and highwaymen lurk among the rocky crests and scrub, ambushing unwary travelers and keeping watch over the approaches to their strongholds below. Beyond mortal outlaws, the Dagger Hills carry an eerie reputation under cover of darkness. The unsettling specter Leprithus, a wandering ghoul, has been sighted stalking the desolate heights when the sun dips. Aside from wandering outlaws and lingering undead, the only resident willing to brave the ridge is Grimbooze Thunderbrew, a dwarven brewer who sets up his distilling equipment in the rugged wilderness regardless of the nearby dangers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dagger-hills-866fe83692358a4c4349/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.0416327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darkshire"] = {
  hash = "0577e8",
  fullHash = "cb395e",
  text = "Darkshire. Darkshire, formerly known as Grand Hamlet, is a battered settlement in eastern Duskwood. Cloaked in an enduring, unnatural gloom and neglected by Stormwind, the town survives only through the vigilance of the Night Watch and the stubborn endurance of its people. Once a peaceful woodland settlement called Grand Hamlet, the village was razed by the Old Horde during the First War and later rebuilt. Its fortune turned truly bitter following the fall of Medivh at Karazhan, whose lingering death throes cursed Brightwood into the shadowy wilderness now called Duskwood. Despite being a nominal holding of the Kingdom of Stormwind, the town hall receives virtually no aid from the crown. Political paralysis in Stormwind City has left the garrison unstaffed and the borders unpatrolled, forcing Mayor Ello Ebonlocke and the local council to administer their own harsh, insular justice in the shadows. Surrounded by walking dead from the western graveyards and feral worgen hunting through the woods, Darkshire clings to life along its palisades. The town's primary shield is the Night Watch, a voluntary civilian militia commanded by Althea Ebonlocke. Operating out of the town square and patrolling the pitch-black thoroughfares, these weary sentries keep the town's roads open and turn back the terrors prowling the tree line. Travelers arriving at the Scarlet Raven Tavern find the locals suspicious, frightened, and desperate for any mercenary capable of holding a blade.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darkshire-65cc60e50e554824210d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.9877551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:demon-fall-canyon"] = {
  hash = "7f8c0a",
  fullHash = "c921ef",
  text = "Demon Fall Canyon. Demon Fall Canyon is a scarred, fel-corrupted gorge situated in southern Ashenvale. It marks the legendary battleground where Grommash Hellscream and Thrall confronted the pit lord Mannoroth, ending the demonic blood curse that had enslaved the orcish race for a generation. Carved into the rugged southern borders of Ashenvale near Felfire Hill and Demon Fall Ridge, this canyon bears the heavy scars of the Third War. It was here that Warchief Thrall and chieftain Grommash Hellscream tracked down the pit lord Mannoroth to break his hold over the Horde. During the fierce duel, Mannoroth incapacitated Thrall, but Grom seized the opening to drive his axe, Gorehowl, straight into the demon's chest. The resulting infernal explosion shattered the pit lord and slew Grom as well, permanently liberating the orcs from their lingering demonic servitude. Following Mannoroth's demise, the Horde dubbed the canyon to commemorate Grom's final stand, erecting a monument nearby to honor his redemption. Despite the pit lord's destruction, his violent death unleashed raw demonic power that thoroughly tainted the surrounding land. Today, the canyon remains saturated in fel magic and crawls with hostile lingering lesser demons, making any pilgrimage to this hallowed ground a perilous venture.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_demon-fall-canyon-5026bdba21fd66e1931c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.5387755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:demon-fall-ridge"] = {
  hash = "e9a7af",
  fullHash = "66ada5",
  text = "Demon Fall Ridge. Demon Fall Ridge is a blighted highland in south-eastern Ashenvale, overlooking the canyon where Mannoroth fell. Swarming with lesser demons and corrupting magic, the ridge centers around a desecrated night elf barrow den claimed by the Burning Legion. Rising directly west and above Demon Fall Canyon, Demon Fall Ridge is stained with lingering fel residue from the Third War. While the canyon below marks the site of the catastrophic clash between Grommash Hellscream and the pit lord Mannoroth, this high ground serves as a staging area for surviving demonic invaders. Lesser demons patrol the rocky cliffs, defiling the ancient woodland and threatening any travelers traversing the southern border of Ashenvale. Carved into the side of the ridge rests an abandoned night elf barrow den, hollowed out and converted into a stronghold of dark sorcery. Rather than slumbering druids, the subterranean passages teem with fel fiends. Deep in the subterranean depths lurks Diathorus the Seeker, a cunning dreadlord who rules over this conclave, directing the remaining demonic remnants throughout the forest.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_demon-fall-ridge-8545247b0f6643a75470/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.5902041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:demont-s-place"] = {
  hash = "2cfc57",
  fullHash = "985f83",
  text = "Demont's Place. Demont's Place is a ruined homestead nestled in southwestern Westfall, just west of the forsaken town of Moonbrook. Once a working agricultural plot, the farm was driven to ruin alongside the rest of the region and claimed by the rogue agents of the Defias Brotherhood. Today, its weathered fences and empty fields stand as another casualty of the realm's fading prosperity. Situated within sight of Moonbrook and the rotting expanse of the Dead Acre, Demont's Place was once among the private family holdings feeding the Kingdom of Stormwind. Economic collapse and the sudden rise of outlaw brigands stripped the farm of its livelihood, forcing out its original caretakers. Much like its neighboring fields, the plot has degenerated into a rough hideout, overrun by outlaws using its weathered grounds as an extension of the Defias stronghold in Moonbrook. Because of its close proximity to Moonbrook, Demont's Place serves the Defias Brotherhood as an operational outpost on the outskirts of their regional center. Thugs, cutpurses, and displaced laborers linger among the fences and shattered structures, ambushing travelers who wander off the southern roads. Until Moonbrook is reclaimed and the outlaws broken, the ground remains far too dangerous for peaceful farming to ever return.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_demont-s-place-451068890d4b44e48939/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.8783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dormitory"] = {
  hash = "32660d",
  fullHash = "54d7ee",
  text = "Dormitory. The Dormitory is a residential sector within the fallen technological capital of Gnomeregan. Once providing living quarters for countless gnome citizens, it now lies choked with radioactive fallout and crawls with savage, irradiated troggs. Situated just past the decontamination chambers of the Clean Zone, the Dormitory formerly housed the inventors, laborers, and families of Gnomeregan. When High Tinker Mekkatorque initiated the emergency radiation venting to combat the invading trogg horde, this densely populated sector became a trap. Today, its communal halls and living units are completely abandoned by gnomish life, occupied instead by mutated troggs that thrive in the radioactive miasma. Travelers descending toward the lower levels of the city must fight through this tragic reminder of the catastrophe to reach the lower Hall of Gears. Amid the abandoned living quarters, remnants of the city's administrative apparatus still function. The Matrix Punchograph 3005-B machines process security punch cards. These terminals provide clearance for higher-security data terminals deeper within Gnomeregan, helping recover important records left behind when the gnomes fled.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dormitory-3e004dc49ed1af84878e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.4636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dun-algaz"] = {
  hash = "396c30",
  fullHash = "9d110e",
  text = "Dun Algaz. Dun Algaz is the fortified mountain pass and network of tunnels connecting Loch Modan to the Wetlands. Historically contested during the Second War, this vital trade artery has recently fallen under siege by Dragonmaw orcs, cutting off easy transit between Ironforge's territories and Menethil Harbor. Dun Algaz predates the War of the Three Hammers, originally serving as the passage the Wildhammer clan used to migrate north after being driven out of Ironforge. Carved deep into the jagged peaks separating Loch Modan and the Wetlands, the pass consists of sweeping stone tunnels, bastions, and watchposts. Its strategic value cannot be overstated: whoever holds Dun Algaz commands the only overland highway linking the dwarven heartlands to the western sea at Menethil Harbor. During the Second War, the Old Horde retreated to Dun Algaz after their defeat at Dun Modr, turning it into their final southern bulwark against Sir Anduin Lothar's forces. Years later, Nekros Skullcrusher sheltered the Dragonmaw clan in the surrounding caves. Though the Alliance reclaimed the pass for a time, Dragonmaw orcs have once again overrun the stronghold, forcing the dwarven Mountaineers to retreat to the upper tunnels and turning the mountain corridor into a deadly gauntlet for travelers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dun-algaz-f09c3d86d0adfd1ce880/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.2367347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dun-garok"] = {
  hash = "53341e",
  fullHash = "cc52c2",
  text = "Dun Garok. Dun Garok is a fortified dwarven mountain bastion situated in the southeastern heights of the Hillsbrad Foothills. Commanded by Captain Ironhill, the outpost's stalwart garrison defends regional Alliance interests and nearby operations like the Azurelode Mine against hostile encroachment. To the Forsaken of Tarren Mill, this entrenched dwarven stronghold stands as a constant military threat bordering their domain. Carved into the craggy southeastern hills of Hillsbrad Foothills, Dun Garok stands as a heavily defended redoubt of Ironforge mountaineers and riflemen. Commanded by Captain Ironhill, the dwarves here maintain a critical defensive perimeter, shielding human settlements in Hillsbrad and securing vital operations at the Azurelode Mine. Their sturdy stone architecture and deep entrenchments provide the Alliance with an anchor against nearby threats. Because of its close proximity to Tarren Mill, Dun Garok is viewed by the Forsaken as an intolerable intrusion on their southern border. Sylvanas Windrunner's forces loathe the presence of heavily armed dwarven troops so close to their stronghold, routinely plotting to infiltrate the fortress, break its defenses, and eliminate Captain Ironhill's garrison to secure Forsaken dominion over Hillsbrad.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dun-garok-2e98dcf2e433cb8e73a4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.6171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dun-modr"] = {
  hash = "5c6f6a",
  fullHash = "050054",
  text = "Dun Modr. Dun Modr is an ancient fortified dwarven town in northern Wetlands, guarding the southern approach to the Thandol Span. Once a critical fortress held and contested across multiple wars, it has recently fallen to a hostile force of Dark Iron dwarves. Situated at the northern edge of the Wetlands, Dun Modr commands the primary overland highway linking Khaz Modan with the northern human kingdoms. Because of this vital location, the stronghold has seen heavy bloodshed over the decades. During the Second War, the Orcish Horde captured and entrenched themselves within the dwarven fortress, using it as a forward staging ground to launch raids and repel Alliance armies. Stromgarde warriors launched a brutal amphibious assault from Tol Barad to reclaim it, only for the Orcs to strike back in a bloody tug-of-war. Alliance forces led by Lord Anduin Lothar ultimately drove the invaders south across the Thandol Span, restoring dwarven custody over the bastion. With Ironforge's attention divided and many of its finest mountaineers committed to Alliance duties abroad, Dun Modr was left vulnerable. Seizing the opportunity, Dark Iron invaders pushed north and sabotaged the Thandol Span with explosives before swarming the fortress. Under the command of the Dark Iron warlock Balgaras the Foul, these infiltrators overran the town's defenders, driving out the Bronzebeard garrison and transforming the strategic outpost into a hostile den. Surrounding Mountaineers now maintain a grim perimeter just down the road, desperately seeking aid to dislodge the occupiers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dun-modr-bc5469fc66503fd09639/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.5820408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:durnholde-keep"] = {
  hash = "e5a6ac",
  fullHash = "e2bf92",
  text = "Durnholde Keep. Durnholde Keep is a shattered fortress situated east of Tarren Mill in the Hillsbrad Foothills. Once the nerve center of the Alliance's orc internment camps and the place where Warchief Thrall was raised as a captive gladiator, it now lies in ruin, occupied by outlaws of the Syndicate. Built by the Alliance, Durnholde served as the headquarters overseeing the southern internment camps following the Second War. Commanded by the ambitious and corrupt Lieutenant General Aedelas Blackmoore, it functioned as both an administrative fortress and a gladiatorial arena. It was here that Blackmoore raised the orphaned infant Go'el, naming him Thrall and training him to fight for sport and profit. Aided by Taretha Foxton, Thrall escaped captivity and later united the dispersed orc clans under the banner of the new Horde. Returning to Durnholde, Thrall laid siege to the fortress, slew Blackmoore, and called upon the earth spirits to shatter its walls. Since its collapse prior to the Third War, Durnholde Keep has remained a scarred heap of masonry. In the vacuum left by Lordaeron's fall, the aristocratic bandits of the Syndicate took refuge among the ramparts. Operating out of the broken bailey and towers, these dispossessed Alterac nobles and cutthroats prey upon travelers across the Hillsbrad Foothills, turning a former instrument of martial law into a heavily fortified rogue outpost.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_durnholde-keep-d2336414eb4f90f28a4d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.6106122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dwarven-district"] = {
  hash = "55fdc5",
  fullHash = "6ad9e7",
  text = "Dwarven District. Tucked into the northern corner of Stormwind City, the Dwarven District is a bustling industrial enclave filled with roaring furnaces, ringing anvils, and thick smog. Established by Ironforge craftsmen who arrived to help rebuild the capital after the Second War, it serves as the beating mechanical heart of the city. After the Second War, King Magni Bronzebeard sent hundreds of dwarven artisans, stonemasons, and engineers south to assist their human allies in rebuilding Stormwind. Rather than dispersing throughout the capital, they established their own enclave in the northern quarter. Today, the district retains the unmistakable character of Ironforge, dominated by heavy stone masonry, sprawling smelting operations, and bustling craft shops. Shops such as Potts' Plates provide a familiar haven for craftspeople from Khaz Modan. It also houses the Stormwind portal to the Deeprun Tram, tethering the human capital directly to Ironforge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dwarven-district-1428dc25cd371e6eba53/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.1036735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:eastvale-logging-camp"] = {
  hash = "2af34c",
  fullHash = "5cce19",
  text = "Eastvale Logging Camp. Eastvale Logging Camp is a vital lumber settlement tucked into the eastern woodlands of Elwynn Forest, near the border with the Redridge Mountains. The camp supplies vital timber to Stormwind and surrounding settlements, but its operations are constantly hampered by hungry predators prowling the surrounding forests. Orders for timber constantly stream into Eastvale Logging Camp from buyers across the Kingdom of Stormwind, but meeting these strict deadlines is a harrowing task. The dense wilderness surrounding the camp swarms with vicious wolves and prowling bears that routinely chase lumberjacks away from their worksites, leaving valuable cut timber abandoned in the woods. Under the direction of Supervisor Raelen, the camp frequently relies on wandering adventurers to cull the encroaching beasts and retrieve fallen wood before deliveries fall fatally behind schedule. Despite its remote location, Eastvale is more than just a logging outpost. It features several residential dwellings and bustling horse stables where travelers can purchase fine mounts. The lumber trade here is closely tied to Stormwind nobility, including Lord Joran Tremaind, whose family maintains significant business interests in the camp's timber harvest. On a nearby hill overlooking the worksite sits the quiet manor of retired Marshal Haggard, the former leader of the Stormwind Guard, who withdrew to this rustic outpost to live out his retirement away from capital politics.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_eastvale-logging-camp-f702943002e9b0689144/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.4538776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:echo-ridge-mine"] = {
  hash = "3ecc87",
  fullHash = "ad842f",
  text = "Echo Ridge Mine. Echo Ridge Mine is a small excavation carved into the northwestern cliffs of Northshire Valley. Rich in stone and ore, it has recently fallen into the clutches of a destructive kobold infestation, halting Northshire's local operations. Tucked away in the hills above Northshire Abbey, Echo Ridge Mine was meant to supply the valley with useful stone and metal. Instead, opportunistic kobolds swarmed through the tunnels, driving out the local workers. Marshal McBride views the infestation as an unacceptable security risk to Northshire's safety and recruitment efforts, directing new recruits straight into the tunnels to cull their numbers and reclaim the site. The occupation of Echo Ridge is part of a broader pattern of kobold encroachment spreading across Elwynn Forest. If left unchecked in the northwestern ridge, these subterranean pests threaten to disrupt Northshire's peaceful training grounds and choke off crucial resources from reaching the abbey.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_echo-ridge-mine-5b2f21b2abd4247f3b63/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.7232653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:engineering-labs"] = {
  hash = "4439e1",
  fullHash = "677e35",
  text = "Engineering Labs. The Engineering Labs are a multi-tiered research and manufacturing facility deep within Gnomeregan. Connected to the Workshop Entrance and Launch Bay, this bustling center of gnome innovation was overrun during the city's radioactive cataclysm and is now swarming with crazed leper gnomes and rogue mechanical contraptions. Built around a central mechanical structure, the Engineering Labs span two primary tiers connected by a central cargo lift. In Gnomeregan's prime, tinkerers here developed schematics and fine-tuned clockwork inventions. Today, radiation leaks have driven the remaining researchers mad, transforming them into deranged leper gnomes who mindlessly guard malfunctioning machinery alongside rogue constructs. Beyond its historical role as a laboratory, the chamber serves as a vital transit hub through the lower reaches of the ruined city. Passages wind toward the Launch Bay, Tinkers' Court, and the Workshop Entrance back door. The lower floor also houses the Matrix Punchograph 3005-D terminals, critical processing units required to decipher the coded punch cards carried by gnome engineers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_engineering-labs-007380b8704f399ec5b4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.355102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:falfarren-river"] = {
  hash = "1b1656",
  fullHash = "d25b47",
  text = "Falfarren River. The Falfarren River flows northeast to southwest through the heart of Ashenvale, originating on the slopes of Mount Hyjal before slipping underground beneath the Barrens' northern mountains. It forms a natural boundary between night elven domains around Astranaar to the west and the encroaching Horde outposts to the east. Travelers must tread carefully, as its shaded banks and shallows harbor hostile wildlife and mark the edge of open conflict. Feeding down from Mount Hyjal, the waters of the Falfarren cascade across waterfalls into Xavian before winding between Splintertree Post and Night Run. While it is neither exceptionally deep nor impassable under ordinary weather, it serves as the rough geopolitical dividing line in central Ashenvale. To the west lie Astranaar and the core holdings of the Kaldorei; to the east sit the camps and lumber operations of the Horde. Crossing the main road's wooden bridges brings travelers into the contested heart of the forest, where patrols clash along the banks. Beyond the tensions of warring sentinels and grunts, the river conceals natural and unnatural dangers beneath its surface. Downstream near Felfire Hill, corrupted bog beasts and aggressive water creatures prowl the muddy shallows. Rainfall can swell the currents, yet the true peril lies in the dense canopies flanking either shore, where ambushes and skirmishes routinely spill into the stream.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_falfarren-river-282292be9ba164296765/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.1477551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fargodeep-mine"] = {
  hash = "c12ebb",
  fullHash = "0c5849",
  text = "Fargodeep Mine. Fargodeep Mine is an abandoned gold mine nestled in central Elwynn Forest, south of Goldshire. Once a productive source of wealth for the Kingdom of Stormwind, it has recently been overrun by a belligerent clan of kobolds. Fargodeep Mine once provided steady gold to Stormwind's treasury, but the Kingdom's dwindling military presence in Elwynn has left outlying resources vulnerable. Kobolds quickly swarmed into the dark tunnels, establishing a bustling warren beneath the hills. Marshal Dughan and the local authorities in Goldshire view the infestation with deep concern, not only because of lost ore, but because the emboldened creatures now threaten nearby farmsteads like the Stonefield and Maclure holdings. The mine spans multiple levels, with the lower shafts winding deep beneath the forest floor. Kobold laborers and vermin scurry along the wooden scaffolds, fiercely guarding their scavenged wax and gold dust. Deeper within lurks Goldtooth, a particularly cunning kobold leader known for hoarding trinkets pilfered from unwary travelers and surrounding residents.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fargodeep-mine-190865eba55ac7d7e0d9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.8620408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:farstrider-lodge"] = {
  hash = "3b8cf6",
  fullHash = "3519fa",
  text = "Farstrider Lodge. Farstrider Lodge is a hunting outpost in southeastern Loch Modan. Its architecture appears to be high elven, while its residents include the high elf Vyrin Swiftwind, dwarves, and humans. Hunters and travelers come here for training, supplies, and work in the surrounding wilds. Farstrider Lodge stands apart from the sturdy stone buildings typical of Khaz Modan. Its architecture suggests high elven craftsmanship. Vyrin Swiftwind is the only known high elf still residing here, alongside dwarves and humans. The lodge's founder and date of construction are not established. Situated in the southeastern hills of Loch Modan, the lodge serves hunters and travelers exploring the surrounding wilderness. Hunter and pet trainers work here alongside merchants and people seeking help with local wildlife.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_farstrider-lodge-9401434bc79e393c7542/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.0244898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fel-rock"] = {
  hash = "2d904e",
  fullHash = "b574cf",
  text = "Fel Rock. Fel Rock is a blighted cavern hidden within the woods just north of Dolanaar on the boughs of Teldrassil. Once a tranquil hollow, it has fallen into corruption under the influence of the satyr Lord Melenas and his horde of vicious grells. Tucked away in the forested hills north of Dolanaar, Fel Rock was once an ordinary, peaceful cavern carved into the wood and stone of Teldrassil. Today, it stands as an unsettling reminder of the creeping taint encroaching on the world tree. Twisted roots and unnatural shadows dominate the cavern interior, overrun by swarms of wild grells and grellkin. The corruption within Fel Rock stems from the presence of Lord Melenas, a wicked satyr who has claimed the hollow as a stronghold. From deep within the cave, he orchestrates the spread of malevolent corruption right under the watchful gaze of the night elf sentinels. Druids from Dolanaar, particularly Tallonkai Swiftroot, actively enlist capable defenders to cleanse the cave and slay Melenas before his foul blight can spread further into the surrounding woodlands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fel-rock-17329df516b52d7837cc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.235102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:felfire-hill"] = {
  hash = "5bf1c2",
  fullHash = "1791bc",
  text = "Felfire Hill. Felfire Hill is a blighted, demon-infested rise nestled in eastern Ashenvale. Struck by falling infernals during the Third War, its corrupted soil still burns with demonic power. The Horde maintains fortified guard posts on the bridges leading into the hill to safeguard their nearby settlements. During the cataclysmic invasion of the Third War, flaming meteors fell across Kalimdor as the Burning Legion unleashed its dread infernals. Several of those fiery behemoths struck this ridge in Ashenvale, scouring away the ancient woodlands and corrupting the earth itself. Today, the scorched landscape remains saturated with fel energy and prowled by lingering demons who survived the Legion's defeat at Mount Hyjal. Situated southeast of Splintertree Post and southwest of the Warsong Lumber Camp, Felfire Hill sits uncomfortably close to vital Horde operations. Because the demonic remnants threaten to spill out into the surrounding forest, Horde forces have erected fortified barricades and guard towers at both bridges approaching the hill, containing the foul creatures within their blighted haven.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_felfire-hill-9b730f50feb05d385cb5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.8285714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fire-scar-shrine"] = {
  hash = "e943ae",
  fullHash = "0a66dd",
  text = "Fire Scar Shrine. Fire Scar Shrine is a despoiled night elf shrine nestled in the southwestern forests of Ashenvale. Once a serene place of worship, it has been corrupted by demonic forces and turned into a blister of fel energy right on Astranaar's doorstep. Located southwest of Astranaar and west of the Ruins of Stardust, Fire Scar Shrine stands as a grim reminder of the Burning Legion's lingering poison in Ashenvale. The shrine has fallen under the sway of demonic corruption, nurtured by servants of the shadowy Cult of the Dark Strand and the Shadow Council. Fel magic has twisted the surrounding earth into scorched rock, where roaming demons now gather in open defiance of the forest's defenders. The shrine's descent into darkness was spearheaded by agents like the orcish warlock Ilkrud Magthrull, operating under the orders of Athrikus Narassin. Though mortal sorcerers brought the corruption to bear, summoned fiends now patrol the altar stones unchecked. The localized tear in the veil occasionally draws greater horrors, including the towering doomguard Akkrilus.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fire-scar-shrine-65ea6e1577b5423f800a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.2016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forest-s-edge"] = {
  hash = "1c78d7",
  fullHash = "bf1a20",
  text = "Forest's Edge. Forest's Edge is a wooded wedge of land along the southwestern frontier of Elwynn Forest, flanked by rivers leading toward Westfall. Guarded to the north by Westbrook Garrison, this untamed area has become dangerously overrun by riverpaw gnolls under the command of the notorious Hogger. Tucked southwest of Stormwind City between Mirror Lake Orchard and the rivers bordering Westfall, Forest's Edge forms a natural peninsula of woodland. Despite its proximity to Westbrook Garrison, the Stormwind army's thin ranks have left the area vulnerable to wild beasts and raiding parties. Wandering boars roam among the trees and along the riverbanks, but the true menace is the concentration of Riverpaw gnolls who have made these woods their staging ground. The woods of Forest's Edge are the hunting grounds of Hogger, a massive and brutal gnoll chieftain. With a sizable bounty placed on his head by the Stormwind Army, Hogger directs his pack in harassing travelers and raiding nearby farms, turning what could be a peaceful timberland into a lethal proving ground for young Alliance adventurers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forest-s-edge-bde36244662314bf0205/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.6269388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forest-song"] = {
  hash = "df6ccc",
  fullHash = "d715e1",
  text = "Forest Song. Forest Song is an ancient night elf ruin nestled in northeastern Ashenvale, near the tainted woods of Satyrnaar and the great portal at Bough Shadow. At present, it stands largely deserted save for the vigilant druid Kayneth Stillwind, who watches over the eastern border against emergent threats. Tucked away in the dense woodlands of northeastern Ashenvale, Forest Song was once an elegant settlement of the kaldorei. Today, white stone columns and crumbling archways are overgrown with ivy and wild ferns. Located northeast of Satyrnaar and just north of the encroaching Warsong Lumber Camp, the ruins hold a quiet, strategic overlook along the eastern trails toward Azshara and Bough Shadow. While the woods around it are plagued by demonic corruption and Horde incursions, the ruin itself maintains a haunting, tranquil stillness. Forest Song is currently manned solely by the druid Kayneth Stillwind. From this crumbling outpost, Stillwind keeps a watchful eye on the dark designs of enemies entering the wilderness, including the unsettling presence of the Forsaken in the eastern forests. Though isolated far from Astranaar and lacking garrison fortifications, the sanctuary serves as an important rallying point for Alliance travelers venturing toward the northern road into Felwood or investigating the corruptions seeping across the canopy.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forest-song-9166aa618d44e2770120/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.0767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forlorn-cavern"] = {
  hash = "4f1894",
  fullHash = "bec0b0",
  text = "Forlorn Cavern. Tucked along the northern shores of Ironforge's subterranean lake, the Forlorn Cavern is a dimly lit, secluded district far removed from the city's bustling Great Forge. It serves as a shadowy refuge where thieves, cutpurses, and practitioners of the dark arts conduct their business away from prying dwarven guards. While the rest of Ironforge clangs with anvil strikes and radiates heat from the Great Forge, the Forlorn Cavern is cold, damp, and hushed. Wrapping along the northern edge of the mountain's underground lake, the winding pathways sit comfortably out of sight between the Mystic Ward and the Hall of Explorers. Its gloom makes it an ideal gathering place for those who prefer to keep their affairs discreet. Rogues and warlocks train here alongside vendors serving them, while weapon merchants and fishermen also work beside the lake.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forlorn-cavern-397fbf7b3b924bb80818/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.64, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:frostmane-hold"] = {
  hash = "50006c",
  fullHash = "2a79c1",
  text = "Frostmane Hold. Frostmane Hold is a fortified encampment and cavern complex claimed by the Frostmane ice trolls in western Dun Morogh. Tucked into the crags south of Gnomeregan, it serves as a central staging ground for troll raiders encroaching on dwarven territory. The Ironforge Guard keeps a close watch on the hold to gauge the growing troll threat. Carved into the freezing cliffs of western Dun Morogh, Frostmane Hold consists of rough outdoor structures and a winding cave system. For the Frostmane tribe, it functions as a primary stronghold, densely guarded by hideskinners and headhunters. From here, the trolls launch raids into the surrounding valleys, testing the defenses of nearby settlements like Kharanos and menacing travelers along the mountain passes. As tensions in Dun Morogh mount, dwarven commanders in Kharanos have grown increasingly concerned about the scale of the Frostmane buildup. Young mountaineers and adventurers are routinely dispatched to scout the interior caverns, map out the defenses, and cull the warbands before the tribe can muster an organized assault against dwarven outposts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_frostmane-hold-63e99dd0ddf0537f5e18/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.3583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:furlbrow-s-pumpkin-farm"] = {
  hash = "49ec78",
  fullHash = "55f7a3",
  text = "Furlbrow's Pumpkin Farm. Furlbrow's Pumpkin Farm is an overrun homestead in northern Westfall, just off the main road from Elwynn Forest. Once a prosperous plot tended by Farmer Furlbrow and his wife Verna, the fields and farmhouse have been seized by the Defias Brotherhood and prowling harvest golems. Situated near the northern border of Westfall, Furlbrow's Pumpkin Farm was once known for producing robust crops to feed the kingdom. That ended when the Defias Brotherhood swept across the countryside. Outlaws drove Farmer Furlbrow and his wife Verna from their land, leaving their fields overrun with mechanical harvest golems programmed to attack anyone approaching. With the farm buildings occupied by bandits and the fields prowled by renegade machinery, the rightful owners have been forced to camp along the roadside near the Jansen Stead, mourning the loss of their home and livelihood. The farm's fall illustrates the broader collapse of law and order across Westfall. With Stormwind's military garrison spread thin and unable to safeguard rural freeholders, the Defias Brotherhood easily claimed homesteads like Furlbrow's. Bandits use the property as an staging ground and camp while letting lethal harvest watchers guard the perimeter, leaving travelers on the nearby highway vulnerable to ambush.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_furlbrow-s-pumpkin-farm-a6c6b6543b9800add490/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.9567347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:goblin-foundry"] = {
  hash = "79e634",
  fullHash = "bbf212",
  text = "Goblin Foundry. The Goblin Foundry is an industrial workshop deep within the Deadmines where goblin tinkerers manufacture heavy metalwork for the Defias Brotherhood. A massive smelting cauldron dominates the chamber, which remains occupied by goblin craftsmen and engineers under Gilnid. Carved into the subterranean depths of Moonbrook, the Goblin Foundry provides the Defias Brotherhood with heavy manufacturing power. Goblin craftsmen and engineers work among the smelters and iron ramps, producing anchors and cannonballs. Gilnid presides over the foundry's goblin workforce. Craftsmen maintain production while engineers defend the workshop with remote-controlled golems, making its narrow ramps and molten metal dangerous for intruders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_goblin-foundry-dcfa73a9ecc2eb5efd3a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.7330612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gol-bolar-quarry-mine"] = {
  hash = "705c48",
  fullHash = "dfbd05",
  text = "Gol'Bolar Quarry Mine. Gol'Bolar Quarry Mine is an overrun excavation pit deep within the heart of Gol'Bolar Quarry in eastern Dun Morogh. Originally worked by dwarven miners, the tunnels breached subterranean trogg caverns, unleashing a violent infestation that claimed the entire site. Today, the deepest shafts serve as the stronghold for the trogg warband led by the brute Hammerspine. The mine was established by Ironforge dwarves seeking stone and rich ore deposits beneath the craggy peaks of Dun Morogh. Work came to an abrupt, violent halt when pickaxes fractured a wall into an ancient subterranean cavern system inhabited by troggs. Swarming out from the dark, the subterranean beasts overwhelmed the work crews and forced a hasty retreat, ultimately spilling outward to overrun the surrounding quarry pit as well. While younger, weaker troggs roam the sunlit terraces of the quarry basin, the mine itself harbors the warband's fiercest fighters. Deep within these cramped, torchlit tunnels lurks their vicious chieftain, Hammerspine. Reclaiming the valuable quarry remains an urgent priority for the local mountaineers, but driving the infestation out requires venturing into the blackest depths of the mine to cut off the beasts at their source.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gol-bolar-quarry-mine-ba599db9191479e5f577/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.6318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grand-vestibule"] = {
  hash = "a1d5c8",
  fullHash = "67326b",
  text = "Grand Vestibule. The Grand Vestibule is the monumental entrance hall of the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades. Lined with crimson pillars and stone effigies of fallen crusaders, it serves as the threshold where adventurers prepare to breach the monastery's inner halls. Past the heavy outer gates of the Monastery lies the Grand Vestibule, an expansive marble hall dedicated to the martial pride of the Scarlet Crusade. Statues of armored champions stand vigil beside vaulted stone pillars, and zealous sentries, disciples, and augurs patrol the flagstones to strike down anyone daring to enter without the order's crimson tabard. For trespassers, it is the first taste of the Crusade's unrelenting discipline and fanaticism. Because the vestibule sits directly outside the secured inner wings of the monastery, it functions as a perilous gathering point. Both Alliance strike teams sent north and Horde forces pushing out from the nearby Undercity converge under these high arches, turning the ornate entry into a chaotic and bloody battleground long before anyone breaches the inner cloisters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grand-vestibule-c9c48852e395eced8604/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.1902041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:great-forge"] = {
  hash = "7d64f8",
  fullHash = "c25726",
  text = "Great Forge. The Great Forge is the fiery heart and namesake of the dwarven capital of Ironforge. Situated at the very center of the city, its massive furnaces and famed Great Anvil never rest, producing legendary armaments and serving as the bustling center of civic life. The Great Forge was the very first structure built by the early dwarves beneath Ironforge Mountain, and the grand capital grew in concentric rings around it. Molten stone and slag pour constantly from giant foundries into glowing channels, illuminating the cavern with an amber haze. At its center stands the massive Great Anvil, overseen by Master of the Forge Myolor Sunderfury. Renowned masters of metalwork gather around its rim, hammering out armor, tools, and weapons day and night. Beyond its smithing operations, the Great Forge is the bustling crossroads of the entire mountain city. Ringed with guild offices, supply shops, and the city's primary gryphon roost maintained by Gryth Thurden, travelers from every corner of the Alliance mingle amid the clatter of hammers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_great-forge-8eb6f1a93f77d79c624e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.1640816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:great-lift"] = {
  hash = "0d098a",
  fullHash = "aaa58f",
  text = "Great Lift. The Great Lift is a massive, tauren-engineered elevator system perched on the southern edge of the Barrens. It serves as the primary route lowering travelers and supplies hundreds of feet into the sheer canyon basin of Thousand Needles. Despite its strategic value, aggressive quilboar encroaching through the southern wastes have made approaching the rim increasingly perilous. Spanning the sheer cliff edge between the southern Barrens and the canyon floor of Thousand Needles, the Great Lift stands as a marvel of practical tauren construction. Built using heavy timber, braided rope, and counterweighted pulleys, its paired platforms carry caravans, couriers, and warriors safely across a vertical precipice that would otherwise break any wagon. At the top and bottom platforms, tauren like Brave Moonhorn and Grish Longrunner maintain watch, keeping travelers informed of the road ahead and ensuring cargo passes smoothly. Operating the lift has become increasingly hazardous due to the hostile territory above it. Aggressive quilboar tribes in the southern Barrens have expanded aggressively southward, choking the road toward the lift and threatening to cut off Thousand Needles from the northern trails entirely. Those who make the long journey south must constantly fend off razorfen raiders before they can ever step foot onto the wooden platforms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_great-lift-90122f9e1ba85cd63668/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.5461224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:greatwood-vale"] = {
  hash = "3d4c9e",
  fullHash = "6e67ed",
  text = "Greatwood Vale. Greatwood Vale is a forested valley nestled in the eastern foothills of the Stonetalon Mountains, reached via the perilous Webwinder Path. Recently, the hostile Grimtotem tribe seized the vale, using it as a staging ground for bloody assaults on neighboring settlements. Tucked away off the Webwinder Path, Greatwood Vale has become a violent frontier. The Grimtotem tribe, under the ruthless leadership of Grundig Darkcloud, has moved into the vale in force, cutting off safe passage and bringing terror to the surrounding foothills. The Grimtotem did not merely occupy Greatwood Vale; they used it to orchestrate the butchery of nearby Camp Aparaje. Sweeping out of the woods, Darkcloud's raiders slaughtered nearly every tauren inhabiting the encampment. Makaba Flathoof, one of the few survivors who managed to flee the carnage, now directs vengeful Horde warriors straight into the heart of Greatwood Vale to cut down the renegade Grimtotem and put an end to Grundig Darkcloud.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_greatwood-vale-c26d3aa6a5ebe4172355/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.395102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:greenpaw-village"] = {
  hash = "dfe18e",
  fullHash = "fd5b37",
  text = "Greenpaw Village. Greenpaw Village is a furbolg settlement nestled in central Ashenvale, situated between Raynewood Retreat and Silverwind Refuge. It serves as the primary home of the Foulweald tribe, led by Chief Murgut and the shaman Oakpaw. Gripped by demonic corruption and embattled by outside forces, the village is an active conflict point for both Alliance and Horde travelers. Situated south of Raynewood Retreat and northeast of Silverwind Refuge, Greenpaw Village was once a quiet home for the forest's ursine denizens. In the wake of the Third War, its resident furbolgs—the Foulweald tribe—succumbed to the insidious corruption tainting Ashenvale's soil and spirits. Under the dominion of Chief Murgut and their corrupted spiritual guide, Shaman Oakpaw, the Foulweald have become hostile and unstable, attacking any travelers who stray into their forest territory. Because of their relentless aggression and proximity to major roadways, Greenpaw Village draws hostility from every side. Alliance sentinels and night elf druids seek to purge the mad furbolgs to protect their nearby outposts and sacred moonwells. Simultaneously, Horde forces advancing into Ashenvale have fought bloody skirmishes against the Foulweald as both factions push through the ancient woodland.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_greenpaw-village-e0a2f75f930804c6989d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.7216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grim-batol"] = {
  hash = "11ec6a",
  fullHash = "1a68ad",
  text = "Grim Batol. Grim Batol is an ancient, ruined dwarven mountain fortress looming over the eastern edge of the Wetlands. Built by the Wildhammer clan, abandoned after a dark curse during the War of the Three Hammers, and later occupied by the Dragonmaw orcs as a dragon breeding lair, its sealed gates remain an ominous blight upon the highlands. Following the ancient civil war of the three clans, Thane Khardros Wildhammer carved Grim Batol out of the jagged mountains east of the marsh. During the subsequent War of the Three Hammers, the Dark Iron sorceress Modgud laid siege to the fortress. Although Khardros struck her down, Modgud cast a foul shadow across the stones before her death. The lingering taint rendered the halls uninhabitable, forcing the surviving Wildhammer dwarves to abandon their magnificent kingdom and migrate north into the Hinterlands. Centuries later during the Second War, the fortress found a darker purpose when Orgrim Doomhammer dispatched the Dragonmaw clan to claim it. Led by the warlock Nekros Skullcrusher, the orcs utilized the ancient artifact known as the Demon Soul to enslave the red Dragonqueen Alexstrasza deep within the mountain's roots. Forced to hatch broods for the Horde's war effort, Alexstrasza remained chained until a small band of heroes shattered the artifact. Once freed, the enraged Dragon Aspect incinerated the orcs within, leaving Grim Batol a smoldering, desolate ruin watched closely by surviving red dragons.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grim-batol-4858bb45c1995cdddcbe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.8089796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grizzled-den"] = {
  hash = "623b7b",
  fullHash = "9b05ad",
  text = "Grizzled Den. The Grizzled Den is a cavern nestled in the snowy hills just southwest of Kharanos. It serves as a notorious lair for the ferocious, frost-breathing wendigos indigenous to Dun Morogh. Carved deep into the crags southwest of Kharanos, the Grizzled Den is notorious among local mountaineers as the nesting ground of Dun Morogh's wendigo packs. These aggressive, ape-like beasts wield frosty breath against intruders and band together to protect their hollows. Despite the peril, daring hunters regularly brave the cavern's depths to harvest thick wendigo manes, which fetch a respectable sum among craftsmen looking to line cold chairs and carriage seats with insulated fur. Beyond its fearsome inhabitants, the twisting tunnels of the Grizzled Den draw opportunists searching for natural wealth. Prospectors frequently venture past the outer entrance to hammer at copper veins jutting from the frozen stone walls, while adventurous scouts often scour the darker corners for discarded footlockers. However, delve too far into the inner reaches, and you are bound to face the dens' largest and most vicious predators lurking in the shadows.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grizzled-den-6444988f3634e5ad86b8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.075102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grove-of-the-ancients"] = {
  hash = "14330f",
  fullHash = "c34048",
  text = "Grove of the Ancients. The Grove of the Ancients is a sacred night elven sanctuary set amidst old temple ruins in southeastern Darkshore. Nestled beside the Wildbend River, this serene haven serves as a gathering ground for nature spirits and is watched over by the venerable Ancient of Lore, Onu. Tucked away near the southeastern mountains of Darkshore, the Grove of the Ancients stands as a peaceful haven surrounded by aged temple stones. Through its heart flows the surging Wildbend River, providing a quiet refuge amidst a coastline increasingly plagued by corruption and restless wildlife. The grove serves as a meeting place for the Circle of Ancients, where nature spirits and kaldorei tend to the delicate balance of the forest. The focal presence within the grove is Onu, a towering Ancient of Lore who observes the land's shifting fortunes and offers ancient wisdom to passing travelers. Alongside Onu, wandering merchants and slumbering druids find rest beneath the boughs. Though quiet, the grove lies close to territorial furbolg camps to the southeast, making it an essential frontier outpost for Alliance sentinels and druids monitoring southern Darkshore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grove-of-the-ancients-22fe0c1f5e079f9c4a1b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.9183673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gryphon-roost"] = {
  hash = "30bda3",
  fullHash = "4b7344",
  text = "Gryphon Roost. Perched along the ramparts just east of Stormwind's Trade District, the Gryphon Roost serves as the capital's primary transit hub for aerial travel. Managed by the human gryphon master Dungar Longdrink, it houses the majestic beasts that ferry couriers, soldiers, and adventurers across the Eastern Kingdoms. Situated atop the fortifications overlooking the Trade District canal, the Gryphon Roost connects the capital with outlying Alliance garrisons and settlements throughout the Eastern Kingdoms. The flight network is orchestrated by the seasoned master Dungar Longdrink, who coordinates routes to Ironforge, Lakeshire, Sentinel Hill, and beyond. While Stormwind is predominantly a kingdom of men, the presence of gryphons and their handlers brings a bit of Hinterlands tradition directly into the city walls. Gryphons have long been revered partners of the Wildhammer clan, who introduced aerial warfare and transport to the broader Alliance during the Second War. Here on the roost, the salty smell of leather, feed, and fresh winds creates a distinct frontier atmosphere amid the city's stone masonry.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gryphon-roost-4a85de7183760c4ef9b3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.7167347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hall-of-arms-northshire"] = {
  hash = "88749d",
  fullHash = "819ac8",
  text = "Hall of Arms (Northshire). The Hall of Arms is the martial wing of Northshire Abbey, situated south of the Main Hall. Here, veteran instructors drill the newest recruits of the Stormwind Army in the arts of the sword, shield, and holy combat. While the clerics and scholars ponder ancient tomes across the courtyard in the Library Wing, the Hall of Arms rings with the clash of blunted steel and the heavy cadence of martial instruction. Positioned on the south side of Northshire Abbey, this chamber serves as the primary training station for raw human fighters and devout aspirants taking up arms for the Stormwind Army. Here, seasoned battle instructors and paladin mentors teach the basic postures, disciplines, and fundamentals required to defend Elwynn Forest against encroaching kobolds, Defias bandits, and gnoll raiders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hall-of-arms-northshire-fa8103829fac70220bf2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.7110204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hall-of-explorers"] = {
  hash = "9eecf1",
  fullHash = "2e11f8",
  text = "Hall of Explorers. The Hall of Explorers is the sprawling archaeological wing of Ironforge and the bustling headquarters of the dwarven Explorers' League. Part museum, part archive, and part expedition command, it gathers relics, fossils, and ancient tomes from every corner of Azeroth to piece together the forgotten origins of the dwarven race. Occupying the northeastern cavern of Ironforge, the Hall of Explorers—sometimes referred to as the Assembly of Explorers—serves as the nerve center for all Alliance-sanctioned archaeological endeavors. Under the direction of High Explorer Muninn Magellas, scholars and field agents coordinate digs across distant lands, shipping back mysterious relics, ancient titan machinery, and prehistoric bones. The vast Library at its center functions as both a public museum and a restricted storehouse of historical lore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hall-of-explorers-bc915a37a77450523ec2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.1853061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hall-of-gears"] = {
  hash = "6b6d4f",
  fullHash = "9c74aa",
  text = "Hall of Gears. The Hall of Gears is a multi-tiered industrial chamber situated inside the fallen city of Gnomeregan, just past the Clockwerk Run. Once a bustling center of mechanical movement, it has fallen into ruin, flooded with toxic sludge, roaming oozes, and irradiated troggs. Positioned just beyond the Clockwerk Run, the Hall of Gears illustrates the catastrophic collapse of the gnome capital. Divided into two main levels, the chamber's upper catwalks are held by aggressive, irradiated troggs that claw through collapsed access shafts. Below, the floor sits drenched in pools of radioactive fallout, where corrosive slimes and living poison elementals thrive in the toxic runoff. The chamber serves as a critical junction deep within Gnomeregan's operational quarters. From its upper walkways, paths branch toward contaminated dead ends and the relative safety of the Clean Zone and the Dormitory. Descending into the radioactive depths below eventually leads travelers through winding maintenance passages toward the Launch Bay, though navigating either floor requires cutting through the lingering horrors of Sicco Thermaplugg's devastating venting scheme.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hall-of-gears-40f26da7a20e47a67e65/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.0604082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:heroes-vigil"] = {
  hash = "e43720",
  fullHash = "6ed023",
  text = "Heroes' Vigil. Heroes' Vigil is a quiet, sacred clearing situated on the central island within Stone Cairn Lake in eastern Elwynn Forest. Centered around a monument known as the Stone of Remembrance, it was dedicated to honor those who fell during the First War. Today, however, the shrine sits largely neglected by Stormwind and is overrun by dangerous renegade wizards. Erected after the First War, Heroes' Vigil centers upon the Stone of Remembrance, an inscribed stone circle meant to honor the soldiers and citizens slaughtered during the Old Horde's sacking of Goldshire and the surrounding valleys. Though once revered as a sacred bastion of faith and sacrifice, the vigil has gradually slipped from the attention of Stormwind's current rulers, leaving its hallowed grounds overgrown and unattended by the realm's soldiers. Because the kingdom has left the island garrisoned by none, outcasts have seized the memorial. A cabal of rogue wizards now controls Stone Cairn Lake and the monument grounds, practicing wild and undisciplined magic away from the oversight of the Kirin Tor and the Academy of Arcane Sciences. The desecration is made all the more bitter by tragic casualties left near the stones, including the body of Henze Faulk, leaving travelers to wonder if the shrine will ever be restored to peace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_heroes-vigil-ee63341f4687049fbcb2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.2889796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:high-seat"] = {
  hash = "736ded",
  fullHash = "bfc556",
  text = "High Seat. The High Seat is the grand throne room of Ironforge, situated between the Commons and the Great Forge. From this elevated chamber, King Magni Bronzebeard presides over the dwarven realm alongside his royal advisors and historians. The room also houses the sealed passageway leading down into the ancient depths of Old Ironforge. Overlooking the vibrant bustling rings of Ironforge, the High Seat serves as the royal seat of power for the kingdom of Khaz Modan. Within this vaulted chamber, King Magni Bronzebeard hears petitioners, holds council with his advisors, and directs the defense of the Alliance's northern bastions. Flanked by royal dignitaries such as Royal Historian Archesonus and Senator Barin Redstone, the High Seat remains a symbol of unyielding Bronzebeard authority and dwarven resilience amid troubling times across Azeroth. Behind the majestic stone appointments of the throne room lies a heavy doorway leading into the shadowy expanse of Old Ironforge. These lower ruins represent the original, ancestral halls carved out by the dwarves before the city was reconfigured around the Great Forge. Guarded and sealed to general traffic, the portal reminds visitors of the vast antiquity and cavernous history resting beneath the modern capital's stones.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_high-seat-c8d9f796bd968172da44/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.9118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:highperch"] = {
  hash = "f2d6ef",
  fullHash = "a5dd57",
  text = "Highperch. Highperch is an elevated plateau in western Thousand Needles, towering high above the canyon floor. It is renowned as a primary nesting ground for wild wyverns and wind riders. The roost is home to fierce prides defending their young among the windswept crags. Rising sharply out of the canyon floor, Highperch is a rugged sanctuary for Kalimdor's native wyverns. The plateau provides natural defense against predators and an unobstructed launch point for their hunting flights across Thousand Needles. Within its reaches, wyvern prides live under the protection of watchful patriarchs and matriarchs, guarding their nests. The beasts fiercely defend their territory against any intruders who manage to climb into their high domain. While hostile to outsiders, the high cliffs occasionally attract travellers and hunters willing to brave the dangerous ascent. The tauren scout Pao'ka Swiftmountain is stranded on these perilous heights and needs help reaching Whitereach Post. For adventurers journeying through the canyon, scaling the ramp to Highperch means contending with dozens of razor-sharp talons and venomous tails.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_highperch-2b51a7583e23b10a9250/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.1420408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:iceflow-lake"] = {
  hash = "bdc55f",
  fullHash = "65d9ec",
  text = "Iceflow Lake. Iceflow Lake is a frigid, partially frozen body of water situated in western Dun Morogh, directly north of Brewnall Village. Despite the relentless frost, local dwarves maintain fishing holes through the sheet ice to secure fresh water and fish. The snow-covered islands in the center of the lake are prowled by aggressive winter wolves and their alpha, Timber. Spanning western Dun Morogh just outside Brewnall Village, Iceflow Lake battles the bitter Khaz Modan climate year-round. Thick ice sheets continually creep across the surface, forcing local residents like Paxton Ganter to regularly clear holes for ice fishing and drinking water. While the shore provides sustenance, the islands rising from the middle of the lake are far less welcoming. A fierce pack of wild wolves has made these icy mounds their territory, dominated by the notoriously savage gray worg known as Timber.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_iceflow-lake-db4fd1adc70286bebc89/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.7265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:iris-lake"] = {
  hash = "841e73",
  fullHash = "056396",
  text = "Iris Lake. Iris Lake is a blighted body of water resting in north-central Ashenvale, east of Astranaar. Once pristine, its waters have turned a sickly yellowish-green from demonic corruption seeping down from Felwood, and hostile bog beasts now prowl its shores. Nestled between Astranaar and the moonwell tended by Shael'dryn, Iris Lake stands as a grim reminder of the wounds inflicted during the Third War. Fel energies drifting south from the tainted heights of Felwood have poisoned the basin, turning its once-clear waters into a foul, sickly yellow-green mire. The corruption has twisted the native plant life and water spirits, raising aggressive bog beasts that lash out at any traveler who wanders near. Despite the severe pollution choking the lake, sacred remnants of the past still linger along its edges. Rare crystalline stones known as Elune's Tears can sometimes be found resting along the muddy banks. Keepers of elven traditions, such as Shael'dryn, seek these holy stones to aid in purifying tainted moonwells and resisting the spreading demonic blight across Ashenvale.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_iris-lake-af0a8194239cd5d1259b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.5453061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ironband-s-compound"] = {
  hash = "122035",
  fullHash = "366942",
  text = "Ironband's Compound. Ironband's Compound is a fortified outpost tucked into the snowy hills south of Helm's Bed Lake in eastern Dun Morogh. Formerly home to the prospector Sturgy Ironband, the camp has been overrun by hostile Dark Iron dwarves who attack anyone venturing too close. Originally established by Sturgy Ironband, this compound south of Helm's Bed Lake once served as a staging post in the snowy wilderness of Dun Morogh. That changed when Dark Iron infiltrators pushed deep into Bronzebeard territory and seized the site for themselves. Now bristling with hostile dwarves, the compound serves as an enemy forward base threatening travelers and nearby settlements, right under the nose of Ironforge. The presence of a fortified Dark Iron camp within Dun Morogh's own borders is a serious provocation. Ironforge keeps a wary eye on the compound, dispatching seasoned warriors to recover fallen scouts and secure vital intelligence proving the extent of the Dark Iron threat in the mountain kingdom.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ironband-s-compound-082f2bf36c7996637505/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.115102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ironband-s-excavation-site"] = {
  hash = "f20f66",
  fullHash = "57f23b",
  text = "Ironband's Excavation Site. Ironband's Excavation Site is an expansive dwarven dig located in southeastern Loch Modan, just south of the lake and southwest of Farstrider Lodge. Led by Prospector Ironband, the archaeological operation was abruptly halted when aggressive, volatile troggs overran the trenches. Spurred by the dwarven drive to uncover their ancient Titan origins, Prospector Ironband established this sprawling digsite along the rugged cliffs of southeastern Loch Modan. What began as a promising excavation quickly turned into a disaster when the digging disturbed an unusually fierce and large breed of subterranean troggs. The creatures surged from the earth, driving back the miners and setting up camp amidst the scattered tools, broken carts, and half-excavated relics. Much like archaeological efforts in the Badlands, Wetlands, and Uldaman, the site sits in dangerous limbo. Surviving prospectors cling to the edges of the perimeter, desperate to reclaim their stolen gear and protect exposed artifacts—including strange ancient statues left behind by long-forgotten builders—from being smashed or carried off by the ravenous trogg infestation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ironband-s-excavation-site-5671b60b283b894d2808/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.8440816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ironforge-airfield"] = {
  hash = "ba9b56",
  fullHash = "d67a5b",
  text = "Ironforge Airfield. Tucked away high in the snowy peaks northeast of Ironforge, this remote dwarven airstrip serves as a proving ground and staging area for gnome flying machines and dwarven mountaineers. Unmarked by main roads, the plateau overlooks the jagged crags of Dun Morogh and the steep descent toward the Wetlands. Perched far above the beaten trails of Dun Morogh, the high-altitude airfield is a quiet marvel of Alliance cooperation. Sheltered by surrounding mountain peaks, dwarven masonry anchors the blustery ridgelines while gnomish flying machines sit parked along open landing strips. Mountaineers keep watch over the perimeter alongside engineers busy wrenching gears and inspecting steam-belching engines in the biting cold. Despite its isolation, the airfield is far from serene. The surrounding valley features frozen waters and crags contested by Frostmane trolls, who dwell in nearby caves and constantly harass the dwarven sentries. In the shadow of the peaks, dwarven defenders and Stormpike patrols clash regularly with troll raiders across the ice, keeping the remote installation in a state of quiet, vigilant standoff.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ironforge-airfield-fe890da872a250cfb0b9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.9036735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:jansen-stead"] = {
  hash = "f20dd9",
  fullHash = "23fcdb",
  text = "Jansen Stead. Jansen Stead is the northernmost farmstead in Westfall, situated just down the road from the bridge to Elwynn Forest. Like much of the surrounding breadbasket, the homestead has fallen out of peasant hands and is swarming with rogue mechanical Harvest Watchers and roving Defias bandits. Positioned right past the Elwynn border, the Jansen Stead represents the harsh reality greeting anyone traveling westward into Westfall. Once a flourishing family farm feeding the kingdom, its rightful occupants have been driven away by the rising tide of unrest. In their place, hostile Harvest Watchers—mechanical constructs associated with the Defias Brotherhood—patrol the blighted fields alongside brigands. Its proximity to the main road makes it a prominent early hazard for couriers and travelers moving between Goldshire and Sentinel Hill.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_jansen-stead-9769b6d3660ed14a7ce8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.2, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:jasperlode-mine"] = {
  hash = "1f1858",
  fullHash = "14990a",
  text = "Jasperlode Mine. Jasperlode Mine is an iron and mineral excavation in north-central Elwynn Forest that once supplied Stormwind City with vital resources. Today, the shafts are overrun by swarming kobolds and deadly giant spiders, leaving the resource-strapped kingdom unable to reclaim it. Jasperlode Mine was historically one of Stormwind's most productive mining operations, providing steady mineral wealth alongside nearby Fargodeep. With the kingdom's military stretched thin defending against external threats and unrest, local garrisons have been unable to protect the shafts. Kobolds seized the opportunity to burrow inside, driving human miners out and stockpiling stolen gold and tallow candles. The infestation within Jasperlode extends beyond simple vermin. In the deepest, darkest corridors beneath the timber supports, an aggressive colony of mine spiders has taken root. Led by a massive matriarch known to locals as Mother Fang, the spiders treat trespassing kobolds—and any unwary prospectors—as effortless prey, making any attempt to salvage the mine exceptionally hazardous.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_jasperlode-mine-0dda6a2ab8031cb777f8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.4816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:kargathia-keep"] = {
  hash = "b17efe",
  fullHash = "6d4f6e",
  text = "Kargathia Keep. Kargathia Keep is a fortified Horde stronghold anchoring the eastern front of the Warsong Lumber Camp in Ashenvale. Serving as headquarters for the outpost's overseers, it guards the river border and ensures rapid reinforcement lines directly back to Orgrimmar. Perched on the eastern edge of the Warsong Lumber Camp just west of the Southfury River, Kargathia Keep serves as the command center for the Horde's logging operations in Ashenvale. Upgraded with heavy spiked barricades, this fortified outpost houses Overseer Gorthak and his officers. Its strategic position against the river border allows the Warsong clan to monitor incoming incursions and receive swift reinforcements directly from the neighboring lands of Durotar and Orgrimmar whenever night elf skirmishers strike. Like the fortress of Kargath in the Badlands and Bladefist Bay along the coast of Durotar, the outpost takes its name from the famed chieftain Kargath Bladefist. For the Warsong orcs braving Ashenvale's hostile woodlands, the keep's imposing classic architecture stands as an enduring symbol of martial resilience and Horde defiance in territory claimed by the night elves.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_kargathia-keep-636641511d7dfdbe672f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.5004082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:kharanos"] = {
  hash = "ea401e",
  fullHash = "8e1185",
  text = "Kharanos. Kharanos is a bustling mountain settlement nestled in the snowy heart of Dun Morogh, just down the road from Ironforge. Serving as the primary hub for travelers leaving Coldridge Valley, the town hosts a forge, gnomish workshops, and the famous Thunderbrew Distillery. Situated along the central road of Dun Morogh, Kharanos is the first true town welcoming recruits arriving from Anvilmar. Despite the biting cold and biting winds, the settlement thrives thanks to local dwarven grit and gnomish ingenuity. It centers around a roaring public forge and the Thunderbrew Distillery, renowned across the eastern mountains for producing some of the finest ale in Khaz Modan. Between bustling crafters and weary travelers seeking warmth, the town stands as the essential staging ground for business in the lower peaks. Behind its cozy hearths, Kharanos serves as a vital military outpost tasked with keeping the mountain passes secure. Fierce indigenous wendigo prowl the frozen caverns directly to the west, while aggressive Frostmane trolls remain entrenched atop Shimmer Ridge to the northwest. With Ironforge's regular forces stretched thin across the realm, the town relies heavily on visiting adventurers and local watchmen to drive back raiding parties, cull dangerous predators, and ensure the trade routes remain open.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_kharanos-83a83f1c534b5e40766c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.8081633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-al-ameth"] = {
  hash = "222b41",
  fullHash = "ea9d8a",
  text = "Lake Al'Ameth. Lake Al'Ameth is one of the largest freshwater bodies nestled high in the boughs of Teldrassil, located just south of Dolanaar. Though once a tranquil haven featuring little more than a simple wooden jetty, its shores have grown perilous due to the violent agitation of the local plant life. Resting immediately south of the village of Dolanaar, Lake Al'Ameth serves as a vital natural basin in the heart of Teldrassil. Despite its serene waters and minimal settlement, peace here has evaporated. Swarms of agitated timberlings and timberling sprouts prowl the water's edge, driven mad by an unnatural sickness spreading through the world tree. Druids and researchers from Dolanaar frequently dispatch travelers to these shores to gather seeds and cull the aggressive creatures before the corruption spreads deeper into the surrounding forest. The hostility around Lake Al'Ameth is directly tied to the woodland spirits losing their balance. Massive, corrupted treants like Oakenscowl lurk in the nearby groves, while natural timberlings lash out at any who approach. For the Cenarion Circle, diagnosing and treating the rot around Lake Al'Ameth is an urgent test of whether Teldrassil's unblessed creation can truly be sustained.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-al-ameth-00b49f4d21d8e873d187/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.195102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-everstill"] = {
  hash = "1ee5dc",
  fullHash = "3f0c20",
  text = "Lake Everstill. Lake Everstill is the expansive azure body of water dominating the heart of the Redridge Mountains, flanking the town of Lakeshire. Once celebrated for tranquil fishing and clean mountain runoff, its shores and waters have lately turned treacherous due to aggressive murloc tribes and predatory lake beasts. Spanning the central basin of Redridge, Lake Everstill provides the scenic backdrop and vital waterway for the township of Lakeshire. Its crystal-clear waters drain eastward toward the looming heights of Stonewatch. Spanned by the town's central bridge, the lake has long sustained local trade and quiet anglers, though anyone walking the shoreline today will find its peaceful reputation thoroughly shattered by encroaching wilderness threats. In recent times, hostile murlocs have established camps along the northern and eastern shallows, disrupting trade and harrying fishermen. Below the surface, aquatic hazards lurk as well, including dangerous threshers that patrol the deeper waters. Adding to the town's grievances, an errant mage accidentally tore open an unfiltered portal beneath the lake just a year ago in 24 ADP, momentarily draining water into Ironforge and sparking both the Great Lakeshire Drought and the Great Ironforge Flood.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-everstill-9d11790937e1e3dc768f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.0383673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-falathim"] = {
  hash = "bb0a73",
  fullHash = "b5d0b7",
  text = "Lake Falathim. Lake Falathim is a tranquil body of water nestled in the forested hills of western Ashenvale, southwest of Maestra's Post. Revered for the constant touch of Elune's moonlight upon its surface day and night, it was once a beloved retreat for night elves. In recent days, however, aggressive murlocs have overrun its shores, turning a sacred haven into a perilous hazard. Tucked away southeast of the Zoram Strand, Lake Falathim was long cherished by the Kaldorei as a peaceful sanctuary for swimming and fishing. According to local lore, the gentle radiance of Elune rests upon these waters at all hours, day or night. Despite this sacred blessing, ferocious murlocs have surged inland to seize the shores, nesting in thick numbers and driving away local travelers and scholars alike. The sudden occupation by murlocs has made the lake deadly to solitary wanderers. The night elf scholar Teronis was recently lost somewhere near the water's edge on a mission of inquiry. Given the savage hostility of the amphibians lurking on the banks and in the shallows, the Sentinels fear the worst for any who venture toward the moonlit pool alone.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-falathim-2b7f9498985e2088b72c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.075102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lakeridge-highway"] = {
  hash = "0354b7",
  fullHash = "51ad25",
  text = "Lakeridge Highway. Lakeridge Highway is an old thoroughfare running along the southern shore of Lake Everstill in the Redridge Mountains. Once a vital and heavily traveled artery prior to the First War, it has fallen into ruin and disuse. Today, roving packs of gnolls and Blackrock orcs make passage along the road perilous for travelers. Before the devastation of the First War, the Lakeridge Highway served as a bustling trade corridor through Redridge, carrying goods and folk along the southern banks of Lake Everstill. In those peaceful days, travelers could journey between the valley settlements and the eastern heights under the protection of the Kingdom of Stormwind. That safety shattered when the Horde marched through the region, leaving eastern Redridge contested and desolate. Following the seizure of Stonewatch Keep by Blackrock orcs, regular maintenance and patrols along the highway ceased entirely. The road quickly deteriorated into an overgrown track contested by savage gnoll packs. With Stormwind's military spread thin and unable to reinforce Lakeshire, the highway remains officially unsafe. Any traveler seeking to reach the eastern ridges must now carve a path through gnoll ambushes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lakeridge-highway-f1abb90952e55389fdd4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.2693878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lakeshire-inn"] = {
  hash = "920702",
  fullHash = "fd7490",
  text = "Lakeshire Inn. The Lakeshire Inn is the social hub and primary refuge in the beleaguered town of Lakeshire. Situated in the heart of the Redridge Mountains, it provides warmth, ale, and safe lodging for local townsfolk and weary travelers contending with the surrounding perils. Perched on the shore of Lake Everstill, the Lakeshire Inn offers a brief respite from the gnoll bands and Blackrock orcs pressing in on the town. Despite Lakeshire's increasingly desperate military situation, the tavern maintains a cozy, bustling atmosphere. Barkeep Daniels and Innkeeper Brianna attend to the locals and travelers, serving up flagons of ale, wine, and warm meals. It is a vital meeting point where townspeople trade news, drown their sorrows, and seek hired blades to help defend the eastern border of the kingdom. Beyond ordinary patrons nursing drinks at the counter, the inn draws a variety of colorful figures. Wiley the Black watches the room from a table, keeping his own counsel and keeping tabs on regional dangers. Traveling brewers like Yorus Barleybrew make stops here, while Darcy Parker and other townsfolk frequent the common room. For adventurers venturing further into the jagged peaks or heading south toward Duskwood, the inn is the final bastion of civilization and safety before entering contested wilderness.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lakeshire-inn-0c3fe53c5045fa0966d5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.7216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lakeshire-town-hall"] = {
  hash = "6de6ce",
  fullHash = "f78cb9",
  text = "Lakeshire Town Hall. Lakeshire Town Hall serves as the administrative seat of the Redridge Mountains, standing at the heart of the lakeside settlement. Inside, Magistrate Solomon oversees local governance while wrestling with relentless threats from Blackrock orcs and gnolls. Lakeshire Town Hall is an aging stone structure that has been diligently refurbished multiple times over the years. Positioned prominently within Lakeshire, it houses Magistrate Solomon, a kind and dedicated civil leader tasked with maintaining order in the eastern reaches of the Kingdom of Stormwind. With Stormwind's military resources stretched thin and royal aid scarce, the hall has transformed from a quiet administrative office into an active command center where desperate appeals for volunteer defenders and mercenaries are processed daily. Gnoll incursions and the encroaching Blackrock orcs have pushed Solomon to desperation. With aid scarce, the magistrate readily accepts help from independent adventurers who come through the town hall doors.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lakeshire-town-hall-68b5efbec30f6d8073cd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.5853061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:library-ironforge"] = {
  hash = "10e7ed",
  fullHash = "5af0aa",
  text = "Library (Ironforge). Tucked inside the eastern wing of the Hall of Explorers, the Library serves as Ironforge's foremost repository of historical knowledge, archeological relics, and ancient lore. Maintained diligently by the Explorers' League, it houses extensive historical tomes alongside rare artifacts recovered from digs across Azeroth. Occupying a massive chamber within the Hall of Explorers, the Library is where dwarven scholars sift through the relics and field reports recovered by adventurers across the world. Administered by the Explorers' League, the shelves are packed with foundational chronicles detailing the War of the Three Hammers, the ancient Troll Wars, and the origins of the dwarven race. Inquiries regarding rare books or archival locations are directed to Librarian Mae Paledust. Beyond rows of dense bookshelves, the Library doubles as a working museum overseen by Curator Thorius. Here, physical specimens collected from deep excavations, abandoned ruins, and distant shorelines are analyzed, preserved, and displayed. The League's curators preserve everything from crude murloc idols and carved knives to mysterious relics recovered frozen in northern ice, cataloging each find to piece together the hidden history of the Titans and early Azeroth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_library-ironforge-eabcd102adbb7eda266c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.8, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:long-wash"] = {
  hash = "bfb993",
  fullHash = "2a440f",
  text = "Long Wash. The Long Wash is a bleak, windswept stretch of beach in Darkshore running just south of Auberdine toward the ruins of Ameth'Aran. Gloomy and scattered with marine carcasses and flotsam, the shoreline has recently grown dangerous due to an increasing presence of hostile murlocs. Despite its name, the Long Wash is actually the shortest distinct stretch of coastline in Darkshore. It sits under the region's perpetual overcast skies, an expanse of damp sand littered with flotsam, beached driftwood, and the carcasses of sea creatures washed up by the Veiled Sea. While local crabs comb through the surf, travelers southward from Auberdine must tread cautiously: murloc tribes have established camps along the water's edge, aggressively attacking anyone encroaching on their scavenged territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_long-wash-c40366e2cdccfff76532/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.9461224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lordamere-lake"] = {
  hash = "db632d",
  fullHash = "13635d",
  text = "Lordamere Lake. Lordamere Lake is one of the largest bodies of freshwater in the Eastern Kingdoms, bordered by Tirisfal Glades, Silverpine Forest, and the Alterac Mountains. Once the bustling aquatic heart of human civilization in the north, its cold waters and islands are now haunted by Scourge necromancers, feral murlocs, and the spreading shadow of the undead. In the golden age of the Alliance of Lordaeron, Lordamere Lake sat at the crossroads of human power. The Capital City of Lordaeron guarded its northern shores, the sorcerous city-state of Dalaran rose near its southern reach, and the kingdom of Alterac lined its mountainous eastern edge. Merchant vessels and fishing boats once crossed its broad, mist-covered expanse without fear. Following the devastation of the Third War, the lake was transformed from a hub of commerce into a natural barrier dividing the Forsaken-held lands of Tirisfal and Silverpine from the treacherous heights of Alterac. Today, navigating the dark waters of Lordamere is fraught with peril. At the center of the lake sits Fenris Isle, where Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw and his undead thralls hold out against the Forsaken, while vicious worgen prowl the vicinity. Further east lie the Dawning Isles, completely overrun by aggressive Vile Fin murlocs. From the ruins around Dalaran to the shoreline of the Undercity, the lake is no longer a peaceful highway, but a drowned frontier contested by remnant monsters and the armies of the dead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lordamere-lake-e75601a23f1d7c7570a0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.6971429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:maestra-s-post"] = {
  hash = "fd4768",
  fullHash = "b936b9",
  text = "Maestra's Post. Maestra's Post is a small Alliance settlement in western Ashenvale, perched along the road northwest of Astranaar and south of the Ruins of Ordil'Aran. Guarded by night elven Sentinels and hosting a rare human presence in Kalimdor's ancient forest, it serves as a forward watch against encroaching threats. Positioned under the canopy of western Ashenvale, Maestra's Post consists of a slender night elven tower and an open lodge. It sits as an essential waypoint for travelers marching between the coast of Darkshore and the larger hub of Astranaar. Despite its tranquil elven architecture, the outpost stands perpetually on edge. The local Sentinels keep a wary eye on the surrounding wilderness, where corrupted furbolg stir and nearby Horde movements constantly threaten the Alliance's supply lines. Unlike many insular night elf enclaves deep within the woods, Maestra's Post is notable for housing a handful of human allies. Figures such as Feero Ironhand and Delgren the Purifier dwell alongside the Sentinels here, lending their martial strength and devotion to the holy Light in service to the broader Alliance. Their presence makes this remote campsite one of the earliest meeting grounds between the eastern kingdoms and the ancient defenders of Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_maestra-s-post-c7a5b894c51063ae7882/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.3820408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:malaka-jin"] = {
  hash = "726d21",
  fullHash = "099218",
  text = "Malaka'jin. Malaka'jin is a small Horde encampment nestled in the lower hills of the Stonetalon Mountains, just south of the Grimtotem Post. Inhabited primarily by Darkspear troll hunters, the settlement serves as a rustic waystation and hunting outpost for travelers ascending into the rugged peaks from the Barrens. Tucked away near the main path into the Stonetalon Mountains, Malaka'jin provides a welcome sanctuary for Horde wanderers venturing into contested heights. The village is home to a band of rugged troll hunters who eke out a living off the crags and scrublands. While living modestly, these hunters offer supplies, trade their fresh game to weary adventurers, and keep a wary eye on the hostile mountain wildlife and the belligerent Grimtotem tauren lurking further up the trails. Perched precariously near the boundary of the Barrens, Malaka'jin marks one of the earliest Horde presences established in Stonetalon. Though small and quiet compared to the major fortress at Sun Rock Retreat further north, it functions as a vital logistical anchor, allowing Horde trackers to monitor tribal unrest and secure the winding roads against encroaching threats.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_malaka-jin-c6e5176f9c037bff40e8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.9371429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:master-s-glaive"] = {
  hash = "6349d9",
  fullHash = "c2ceb7",
  text = "Master's Glaive. The Master's Glaive is an ancient, unsettling landmark tucked into southwestern Darkshore where a colossal adamantine weapon stands impaled in a massive petrified skull. Long revered by local night elves as a sacred mystery, the site now swarms with Twilight's Hammer cultists drawn to the lingering malevolence of an ancient entity. Rising out of the Darkshore earth, the Master's Glaive is a massive adamantine blade buried deep in a colossal petrified skull. No record names the creature that died here or the hand that drove the blade home. Onu, the Ancient of Lore, says only that an old, old lord of the earth fell on this spot, and the night elves long believed that only the titans could have wielded so gigantic a weapon. Today, the impaled monolith stands as a stark, petrified monument to that forgotten struggle. Though the site was held in solemn reverence by night elves for generations, sinister forces have recently seized the grounds. Fanatics of the Twilight's Hammer cult have established a camp around the sunken carcass, carving out trenches and uncovering petrified ribs. Drawn by whispers beneath the stone, the cultists obsessively study the remains and commune with the malevolent power that still clings to the ancient remains, seeking to revive or harness whatever fell essence slumbers within the earth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_master-s-glaive-c55b8add6592d76f396f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.875102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:menethil-harbor"] = {
  hash = "274589",
  fullHash = "c0e7a2",
  text = "Menethil Harbor. Menethil Harbor is a heavily fortified dwarven port town perched on an island off the western shore of the Wetlands. Built over an old Orcish shipyard and named after King Terenas Menethil II, it acts as the primary maritime lifeline connecting the Eastern Kingdoms to the distant shores of Kalimdor. Sheltered behind massive stone battlements and the sturdy towers of Menethil Keep, it is a bustling hub for merchants, off-duty soldiers, and salty adventurers. The site of Menethil Harbor first served Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer during the Second War, when the Horde constructed an invasion armada out of timber and iron ransacked from Khaz Modan. After the orcs were routed, Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore oversaw the rebuilding of the anchorage into a premier Alliance haven, naming it in tribute to the late King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron. Despite its remote marshland setting, it remains a heavily defended bastion, reinforced by massive walls, a single bridged causeway, and the four stone watchtowers of Menethil Keep. Today, the harbor is the grand crossroads of the Alliance. Ships steam continually across the ocean toward Theramore Isle in Dustwallow Marsh and Auberdine in darkshore, carrying trade, diplomats, and off-duty Kul Tiran marines blowing their wages at the Deepwater Tavern. Yet peace is fragile here: inland supply paths through Dun Algaz are regularly contested by the Dragonmaw clan, while hostile Dark Iron raiders pressure the northern marshes, leaving Captain Stoutfist and the harbor garrison on constant alert.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_menethil-harbor-9c359d26835d95a33552/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.8171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:military-ward"] = {
  hash = "55c863",
  fullHash = "531695",
  text = "Military Ward. The Military Ward serves as the martial heart of Ironforge, housing the city's command infrastructure, weapons masters, and barracks. Marked by bold red banners, it is where dwarven soldiers, hunters, and siege engineers train alongside mechanized siege engines and flying machines. Situated within the northern perimeter of the mountain fortress, the Military Ward concentrates Ironforge's defensive forces into a bustling staging ground. The central complex, the Hall of Arms, accommodates warrior and hunter trainers, battlemasters, and hangars housing dwarven flying machines and steam tanks. Surrounding the drill squares and training dummies are supply outlets such as Craghelm's Plate and Chain, Goldfury's Hunting Supplies, and Timberline Arms, making the ward the primary staging point for mountaineers and city defenders. Distinguished from the pale blue of the Commons and the fiery orange of the Great Forge, the Military Ward is draped in martial red banners. Between drills and weapons inspections, soldiers and off-duty guards gather at Bruuk's Corner for an ale, while Weapon Master Buliwyf Stonehand instructs recruits in ancient dwarven martial traditions.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_military-ward-21a3ecffa666c60072c9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.1983673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mirage-raceway"] = {
  hash = "105565",
  fullHash = "ab5e2a",
  text = "Mirage Raceway. The Mirage Raceway is an impromptu engineering proving ground situated in the shimmering salt flats of Thousand Needles. Founded on the heated rivalry between goblin tinkerer Pozzik and gnome engineer Fizzle Brassbolts, it pits high-speed rocket cars against one another across the baked earth. The track serves as a neutral gathering place where members of both the Alliance and Horde come to wager, tinker, and cheer on their favored machines. Sprawled across the vast white desert floor of the Shimmering Flats, the Mirage Raceway was born out of an unrelenting grudge match between two master tinkerers: the goblin Pozzik and the gnome Fizzle Brassbolts. Determined to settle once and for all whose race possesses superior technical ingenuity, the two constructed custom rocket-powered drag cars and recruited rival crews to pit them against each other in daily high-velocity showdowns. The relentless competition has turned the barren flats into a bustling hub of invention, sabotage, and testing as each crew desperately looks for any mechanical advantage. The raceway draws supporters from both the Horde and Alliance. Spectators gather from across Kalimdor to cheer on either gnome precision or goblin firepower, while gamblers and shady opportunists thrive on the constant wagers placed on the results. Beyond the roar of rocket thrusters, the camps serve as a busy trading depot for travelers journeying between Thousand Needles and the blazing sands of Tanaris.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mirage-raceway-cb9ca09ddd4f0ba3f88c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.1257143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mirkfallon-lake"] = {
  hash = "6f02da",
  fullHash = "9efc93",
  text = "Mirkfallon Lake. Mirkfallon Lake sits in a central highland basin in the Stonetalon Mountains, nestled beneath the sheer cliffs of Windshear Crag. Its waters and surrounding banks teem with native wildlife, including aggressive slimes, roaming thunder lizards, and descending wyverns. Positioned at the geographic center of the Stonetalon range, Mirkfallon Lake rests between the mountain passes leading south toward the Webwinder Path and north toward Stonetalon Peak. Fed by highland runoff, the lake offers a fertile oasis amidst the craggy peaks, drawing thunder lizards down to drink and wyverns from their roosts high above. The waters are also inhabited by strange, bubbling slimes, turning an otherwise serene mountain tarn into a hazardous crossing for lone travelers. Paths crisscross the lake basin in all directions. To the east, rocky tracks ascend into Windshear Crag and the logging operations running deep into the forested valleys. Travelers pressing north past the lake climb toward night elven sanctums and tauren heights, while those skirting south must contend with venomous spider hollows. As a result, the shores of Mirkfallon Lake often serve as an unpredictable crossing point for explorers and scouts navigating the contested mountains.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mirkfallon-lake-f46ff57842d4f8991d76/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.5053061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mist-s-edge"] = {
  hash = "baff8e",
  fullHash = "f353d3",
  text = "Mist's Edge. Mist's Edge is the long, windswept coastline stretching north from Auberdine along western Darkshore. Shrouded in damp ocean fog, these gray beaches are littered with shipwrecks, colossal sea creature remains, and roving packs of crabs and murlocs. Running along the western edge of Darkshore, Mist's Edge is defined by damp sands, rocky tidepools, and perpetual coastal fog. The beach is a graveyard of the sea, littered with decaying sea behemoths, drift timber, and the shattered ribs of wrecked vessels. While seemingly barren, the coastline teems with shoreline scavengers, notably aggressive crabs and territorial murloc tribes that nest in the surf. The treacherous waters off the coast of Darkshore have claimed countless vessels, depositing shattered hulls and discarded cargo all along the sands. Night elf sentinels and local scavengers keep a wary eye on the coastline, where opportunistic murloc encampments frequently menace travelers heading up and down the coast from Auberdine.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mist-s-edge-b8dd02676f5791aee446/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.7722449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mo-grosh-stronghold"] = {
  hash = "48f704",
  fullHash = "10d5f8",
  text = "Mo'grosh Stronghold. Mo'grosh Stronghold is a fortified den of ogres dug into the northeastern hills of Loch Modan. Nestled among ancient, weathered ruins, these brutal giants pose a constant threat to the dwarven settlements below. Tucked away in the crags of northeastern Loch Modan, Mo'grosh Stronghold serves as the primary base for the Mo'grosh clan of ogres in the region. The brute forces lurking within the caves and outer camps raid traveling dwarven supply caravans and present an enduring danger to the peace of Thelsamar. The rugged stonework scattered around the camp suggests the site once belonged to an older settlement, before the lumbering ogres claimed the territory and raised their distinctive mounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mo-grosh-stronghold-31a6d359e6fa67582260/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.9828571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:moonbrook-schoolhouse"] = {
  hash = "824320",
  fullHash = "88aac5",
  text = "Moonbrook Schoolhouse. Standing in the northwestern corner of Moonbrook, the Schoolhouse is a prominent building resembling a town hall with a small graveyard behind it. Originally erected to educate local children under headmaster Stalvan Mistmantle, it now stands derelict amidst the Defias occupation of Westfall. Before Moonbrook fell to ruin and bandits, the community invested in the education of its youth by raising this grand schoolhouse. After Stalvan Mistmantle began tutoring local children, the mayor commissioned him to run a school. In those brighter days, the hall bustled with students rather than thieves, standing as proof of Moonbrook's former prosperity. Now, the schoolhouse lies abandoned to the elements and infested with outlaws, while the weathered headstones of its rear cemetery silently oversee Westfall's steady decay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_moonbrook-schoolhouse-661b9f3df5dae6823ac9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.684898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:moonspray"] = {
  hash = "0a9b3a",
  fullHash = "e91ac3",
  text = "Moonspray. The Moonspray is an Alliance transport vessel belonging to the Kaldorei Fleet. It provides essential maritime passage across the waters separating mainland Kalimdor from the great World Tree, Teldrassil. The Moonspray serves as an indispensable lifeline between the docks of Auberdine on Darkshore's coast and Rut'theran Village at the root of Teldrassil. Traversing the Veiled Sea, the transport carries adventurers, couriers, and vital provisions between the isolated night elf sanctuary in the crown of the World Tree and the ancient mainland of Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_moonspray-7d2a989095ea14a1d82d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 33.7763265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mor-shan-rampart"] = {
  hash = "9b9d12",
  fullHash = "36ca11",
  text = "Mor'shan Rampart. The Mor'shan Rampart is a heavily fortified Horde barricade positioned at the northern border of the Barrens, guarding the entrance to Ashenvale. Constructed primarily by the Warsong clan, it shields Horde territory from night elf counterattacks while serving as a staging ground for incursions deeper into the forest. Standing at the northern terminus of the Gold Road, the Mor'shan Rampart marks the transition between the arid plains of the Barrens and the ancient canopies of Ashenvale. Built from heavy timber, defensive palisades, and fortified watchtowers, this bulwark was raised to stem the retaliatory strikes of night elven forces outraged by the Horde's logging operations. It acts both as a shield securing the northern gate to Kalimdor's interior and as a forward staging post for raiding parties pushing north toward Astranaar. Manned primarily by battle-hardened fighters affiliated with the Warsong clan, the rampart remains under constant military readiness. From these parapets, sentries monitor the tree line for night elf scouts and archers, keeping open supply lines between the frontline outposts of Ashenvale and the greater Horde settlements to the south.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mor-shan-rampart-7077cc97239c60f6b5b0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.4, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mortwake-s-tower"] = {
  hash = "b098a9",
  fullHash = "929701",
  text = "Klaven's Tower. Klaven's Tower, also called the Defias Tower, is a fortified watchtower standing near the river in southeastern Westfall. Commanded by the rogue Klaven Mortwake, it serves as a critical waypoint for smuggling stolen goods across kingdom borders. Perched on the borderlands north of the Dagger Hills, Klaven's Tower is a vital hub for Defias Brotherhood operations in southern Westfall. Together with Jerod's Landing across the river in Elwynn Forest, the site acts as a receiving post and staging ground for contraband plundered from the Redridge Mountains. Its commanding vantage over the waterways allows the Brotherhood to slip supplies past Kingdom patrols, shielding their logistical network from the crown's eyes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mortwake-s-tower-a37cc66e8adc11364a52/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.3444898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nightsong-woods"] = {
  hash = "a3ca6c",
  fullHash = "1708a3",
  text = "Nightsong Woods. Nightsong Woods forms the embattled eastern expanse of Ashenvale, reaching from Raynewood Retreat toward the borders of Azshara. It serves as the primary battleground between the night elves defending their sacred forest and the Warsong clan harvesting lumber for the Horde. Across its shadowed glades, ancient groves clash with fortified encampments amidst persistent demonic corruption. Stretching eastward across Ashenvale to Azshara, Nightsong Woods is defined by escalating violence. The Falfarren River bisects the western reaches, creating a volatile boundary between Alliance settlements and Horde encroachments. Further east, the forest fractures into heavily disputed territories where Splintertree Post coordinates Horde campaigns against the Silverwing Sentinels. Orcish axes continuously clear southern groves to fuel the Horde's expansion, leaving clear-cut swaths that infuriate the Kaldorei. Adding to the peril, satyr strongholds like Satyrnaar and Xavian, alongside demonic remnants in Demon Fall Canyon and Felfire Hill, taint the eastern canopies.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nightsong-woods-5589b4ada410147074fb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.8734694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northshire-valley"] = {
  hash = "9c92fc",
  fullHash = "71acd9",
  text = "Northshire Valley. Tucked away in the northern mountains of Elwynn Forest, Northshire Valley serves as the peaceful cradle and training ground for the kingdom of Stormwind's newest defenders. Surrounded by natural barriers, it hosts the historic Northshire Abbey, fertile vineyards, and the rich Echo Ridge Mine. Despite its tranquil appearance, the valley faces mounting unrest from rogue bandits, infesting kobolds, and displaced predators. Northshire Valley is framed by nearly impassable mountain ridges, with its only road heading south through a fortified gatehouse toward Goldshire and the rest of Elwynn Forest. A clear river winds through its pastures before feeding south into Crystal Lake. At the valley's heart stands Northshire Abbey, an ancient sanctuary where the Holy Light is taught and where recruits are trained by the Knights of the Silver Hand and seasoned veterans. Beyond the abbey walls lie the bountiful Northshire Vineyards and Echo Ridge Mine, forming a self-sustaining pocket of Stormwind territory that feels far removed from the wider world's wars. Even this sheltered haven has felt the strain of Stormwind's overextended defenses. Marshal McBride and his detachment of the Stormwind Guard struggle to maintain order across the vale. Packs of wolves driven north from blighted Duskwood prowl the surrounding woods, while vermin-like kobolds have overrun the tunnels of Echo Ridge Mine. Worse still, masked thugs aligned with the Defias Brotherhood openly harass the vineyards and threaten the abbey's grounds, forcing local commanders to recruit every able-bodied civilian arriving at their gates.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northshire-valley-a6b61ee4165ba90319b5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.0367347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:old-town"] = {
  hash = "3557ac",
  fullHash = "17aec3",
  text = "Old Town. Old Town is the oldest and grittiest quarter of Stormwind City, having largely survived the destruction of the First War intact. Rather than gleaming stone plazas, its narrow alleys feature weathered brick, working-class tenements, and bustling military facilities. It serves as home to the kingdom's military Command Center as well as the clandestine headquarters of SI:7. While the rest of Stormwind was painstakingly rebuilt from the ground up by the Stonemasons following the First War, Old Town escaped total ruin. Because it did not require wholesale reconstruction, it never received the pristine marble facades and grand canals seen in the Trade District or Cathedral Square. Instead, it remains a rustic, crowded quarter where working folk, laborers, and the city's poorest residents reside. Its cobbled streets are narrower, dirtier, and far more chaotic than the rest of the capital, yet they preserve the authentic atmosphere of pre-war Stormwind. Behind its weathered tenements, Old Town functions as the martial backbone of the Alliance capital. The Command Center houses Stormwind's military officers, barracks, and combat trainers, alongside the Champions' Hall where the Alliance's finest warriors gather. Tucked away toward the district's edge sits the covert compound of SI:7, the kingdom's secretive intelligence agency led by Master Mathias Shaw. Off-duty footmen and shadowy operatives alike gather at the Pig and Whistle Tavern, rubbing shoulders in one of the city's liveliest watering holes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_old-town-f41c11f8bec44ee9a316/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.7044898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:oracle-glade"] = {
  hash = "7a2521",
  fullHash = "d9b0c6",
  text = "Oracle Glade. Nestled in northwestern Teldrassil, the Oracle Glade is a sacred woodland sanctuary home to the wondrous Oracle Tree and the World Tree's premier moonwell. Despite its serene purpose as a center of communion and vigilance, the glade faces constant harassment from encroaching Bloodfeather harpies. The Oracle Glade rests quietly between Darnassus and Wellspring Lake, serving as a vital nexus of night elven communion with nature. At its center stands the Oracle Tree, an ancient specimen whose massive boughs evoke the silhouette of an eagle perched atop a bear. Nearby shimmers the first and most potent of Teldrassil's sacred moonwells, an oasis of lunar magic bathed in Elune's grace. Alongside the Circle of the Ancients, the Oracle Tree watches over the island boughs, shedding inscribed bark that acts as vital communiques to Archdruid Fandral Staghelm regarding the World Tree's health. The glade's tranquility is fragile. Aggressive Bloodfeather harpies have descended upon the grove, establishing roosts among the high branches and threatening the sacred tree. Sentinels dispatched from Darnassus struggle to root out the avian scavengers, who actively harass traveling druids and taint the natural serenity of the moonwell. Travelers passing through the northern boughs must tread lightly, as the harpies strike swiftly at any who venture near their roosts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_oracle-glade-f01a83c82dc24e0a2fc9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.64, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:park"] = {
  hash = "718cba",
  fullHash = "f5847d",
  text = "Park. Tucked along the western edge of Stormwind City between Cathedral Square and the Mage Quarter, the Park is a lush sanctuary of greenery and quiet reflection. Devoted to nature and leisure amidst the bustling stone capital, it serves as the primary haven for visiting night elves and houses the only druid trainers in the Eastern Kingdoms. While the rest of Stormwind is dominated by cobbled walkways, towering limestone ramparts, and crowded market stalls, the Park offers a tranquil breath of wilderness. Originally established as a quiet place of recreation for the city's populace, it naturally transformed into an enclave for night elf emissaries and travelers arriving from across the Great Sea. Sheltered beneath shaded canopies and surrounding a central Moonwell, the district brings a sliver of Kalimdor's sacred glades into the heart of the human kingdom. For the night elves, the Park is a vital foothold in foreign lands. Here, Kaldorei priests such as Nara Meideros tend to visiting pilgrims, and druidic mentors offer their guidance to those devoted to the wild. The district also serves as a peaceful retreat where weary adventurers rest their eyes from the clatter of the Trade District and the solemn duties of the Cathedral.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_park-6a4825973316ec009a9b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.6057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:pig-and-whistle-tavern"] = {
  hash = "ad3b88",
  fullHash = "0dae36",
  text = "Pig and Whistle Tavern. The Pig and Whistle Tavern is a bustling tavern nestled within Stormwind City's rugged Old Town district. It serves as a favored gathering spot for Alliance travelers, local laborers, and weathered veterans recalling the kingdom's past conflicts. Situated amidst the gritty, winding alleys of Old Town, the Pig and Whistle Tavern offers warmth, ale, and lodging to those who walk the capital's rougher quarter. While the nobles of the city congregate in cathedral squares and marble plazas, this establishment caters to common citizens, working folk, and travelers fresh from the road. Inside, patrons can find merchants like the renowned recipe vendor Kendor Kabonka, alongside grizzled veterans who nurse their tankards while trading memories of the First and Second Wars.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_pig-and-whistle-tavern-0849a72310bf4dbbd53c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.1061224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:si-7-stormwind-city"] = {
  hash = "6cccdd",
  fullHash = "0de936",
  text = "SI:7 (Stormwind City). Tucked away in the eastern corner of Old Town, this unassuming stone manor houses the headquarters of SI:7, the kingdom of Stormwind's elite intelligence and espionage service. Behind its heavy doors, spymaster Master Mathias Shaw and his senior operatives quietly monitor threats to the realm, coordinating undercover agents across the kingdom. To the casual passerby in Old Town, this fortified stone structure looks little different from a standard manor or military barracks. In truth, it serves as the nerve center for Stormwind Intelligence, Section 7. Directed by Master Mathias Shaw, SI:7 gathers intelligence, conducts surveillance, and executes covert operations to protect the kingdom from subversion. While the city guard patrols the open cobblestones and the Royal Guard defends Stormwind Keep, SI:7 works in the margins, training handlers and dispatching stealthy operatives against foreign enemies, saboteurs, and domestic unrest alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_si-7-stormwind-city-f40a974348172b246d24/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.5183673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stendel-s-pond"] = {
  hash = "5fe9c2",
  fullHash = "95a4e1",
  text = "Stendel's Pond. Stendel's Pond is a small, possibly tainted body of water located southwest of Sentinel Hill in Westfall. Surrounded by prowling gnolls and marked by nearby graves, it remains one of the few fresh fishing spots in the parched countryside despite its sinister hazards. Tucked away southwest of Sentinel Hill, Stendel's Pond offers a quiet break from Westfall's dusty plains, though danger is never far. Gnolls roam the muddy banks, while scattered skeletons and a pair of lone graves suggest that either the waters or the beasts around it are fatal to careless travelers. Anglers still risk the trek for schools of sagefish.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stendel-s-pond-17136e0b85af2361bef1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.8179592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stonesplinter-valley"] = {
  hash = "ff19b6",
  fullHash = "580255",
  text = "Stonesplinter Valley. Stonesplinter Valley is a rugged mountain vale in southwestern Loch Modan overrun by hostile troggs. Once a pristine alpine hollow, it has transformed into a fortified encampment under the brute command of the trogg chieftain Grawmug. Tucked away among the craggy slopes of southwestern Loch Modan, Stonesplinter Valley was once a quiet alpine hollow. That serenity ended when waves of subterranean troggs clawed their way to the surface across Khaz Modan. Claiming the valley for their own, the Stonesplinter tribe rapidly turned the rocky defiles into crude fortifications, digging dens, erecting barricades, and hunting anything that strays too close. Their numbers make the valley an open wound on roads through southern Loch Modan, forcing Mountaineers out of Thelsamar to hire adventurous mercenaries willing to cull their ranks and eliminate their towering warlord, Grawmug.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stonesplinter-valley-4f9bc3bab7784e2f9e89/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.1330612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stoutlager-inn"] = {
  hash = "95efa6",
  fullHash = "4d4b89",
  text = "Stoutlager Inn. The Stoutlager Inn is the lively heart of Thelsamar and a primary haven for travelers in Loch Modan. Tucked beneath the rolling hills of the loch, it offers warm hearths, strong dwarven ale, and a bustling gathering place for adventurers and locals alike. Situated squarely in the settlement of Thelsamar, the Stoutlager Inn serves as the center of community life for western Loch Modan. Miners, mountaineers, and wayfarers passing between Dun Morogh and the Wetlands stop here to trade rumors, secure lodging, and share hearty drafts of dwarven brew. While the wilderness beyond Thelsamar grows increasingly hostile with trogg and kobold activity, the thick stone walls and roaring fires of the Stoutlager provide a dependable sanctuary.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stoutlager-inn-5dc72ac8ae9a99409da8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.9828571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:the-loch"] = {
  hash = "913e57",
  fullHash = "5a51fa",
  text = "The Loch. The Loch is the vast freshwater lake that dominates the valley of Loch Modan. Held back by the monumental Stonewrought Dam, it is the largest body of water on Khaz Modan and the lifeblood of the surrounding dwarven settlements. Spanning the center of Loch Modan, The Loch is among the largest inland lakes in the known world, rivaled only by Lordamere Lake and Darrowmere Lake in the north. Its waters support local fishermen and provide a peaceful backdrop for the town of Thelsamar and nearby hunting lodges. Beneath its calm surface, however, prowl aggressive threshadons and freshwater beasts that make swimming across a perilous venture. The Loch exists in its current tranquil state thanks to masterwork dwarven engineering. To the north stands the massive Stonewrought Dam, an architectural marvel built across the mountain cleft to prevent the lake from flooding out into the Wetlands below. Maintaining this colossal structure is essential to preserving the valley's ecosystem and keeping Khaz Modan secure.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_the-loch-c2dd4076d2c098507cb8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.2832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thelsamar"] = {
  hash = "7bbe46",
  fullHash = "8ca869",
  text = "Thelsamar. Thelsamar is the primary dwarven settlement in Loch Modan, nestled on the western bluffs overlooking the great lake. Positioned along the main road between Dun Morogh and the Wetlands, it serves as a bustling hub of commerce, hospitality, and civic defense against surrounding trogg and ogre incursions. Thelsamar serves as the central bastion of dwarven civilian life in the valley. Built with traditional Khaz Modan stone and timber architecture, it features the lively Stoutlager Inn, a dedicated gryphon roost, crafting stations, and various supply merchants. Sitting directly on the thoroughfare connecting the South Gate Pass into Dun Morogh with the Algaz Pass toward the Wetlands, almost all travelers through the region pass beneath its warm hearths and stout roofs. Despite its cozy comforts, Thelsamar is in constant danger. To the south, troggs pouring from Stonesplinter Valley threaten the town's perimeter, while bears and predatory beasts haunt Grizzlepaw Ridge. With Ironforge's regular military forces stretched thin across the realm, the local magistrate and Mountaineers rely heavily on traveling adventurers and mercenaries to patrol the trade roads and beat back encroaching hostiles.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thelsamar-a8d71d3620bb9b3e7732/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.3069388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thunderbrew-distillery"] = {
  hash = "4464b9",
  fullHash = "1f7188",
  text = "Thunderbrew Distillery. Thunderbrew Distillery serves as the bustling tavern and central gathering place of Kharanos in Dun Morogh. Renowned far and wide for its potent ales, it functions as both an active brewhouse and an essential rest stop for travelers passing through the snowy mountain passes. Inside, patrons find warm hearths, cold pints, and a wide array of class mentors and local tradesfolk. Perched in the center of Kharanos, the Thunderbrew Distillery is the heart of civic life in southern Dun Morogh. Founded by the Thunderbrew clan—established by Arkilus Thunderbrew and maintained by his kin—the brewhouse is famous across Khaz Modan for producing robust, warming ales built to ward off the biting mountain chill. It operates as the local inn, managed on the floor by Innkeeper Belm, offering weary mountaineers a roaring fire, hearty fare, and beds to escape the blizzards outside. Beyond pouring renowned stouts, the distillery doubles as a vital hub for developing adventurers. Because Kharanos is the first major settlement travelers reach after departing Coldridge Valley, the Thunderbrew tavern plays host to diverse trainers. Mentors in martial arts, holy rites, arcane studies, and roguecraft congregate under its sturdy timber rafters alongside instructors in cooking and emergency medical aid, turning a boisterous drinking hall into an indispensable preparation post.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thunderbrew-distillery-7f109ff0c63fc9a5728d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.7632653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tower-of-ilgalar"] = {
  hash = "42459b",
  fullHash = "42102c",
  text = "Tower of Ilgalar. The Tower of Ilgalar is an isolated spire perched high in the crags of the eastern Redridge Mountains. Once a wizard's retreat, it has fallen into shadow as the hideout of Morganth, a banished sorcerer from Stormwind who now commands the region's feral gnolls and practices dark magic. Looming over eastern Redridge, the Tower of Ilgalar serves as the personal sanctum of Morganth. Cast out from the Mage Quarter of Stormwind for dabbling in forbidden magic, the rogue sorcerer retreated into these rugged heights to delve deeper into the dark arts without imperial interference. Rather than living in solitude, Morganth asserted dominion over the local Shadowhide gnoll clan, employing them as savage sentries around the base of the spire. Inside, the tower crawls with conjured demonic hounds and crackles with shadowy power, posing a constant, brooding menace to the beleaguered town of Lakeshire below. Scholars often draw parallels between the Tower of Ilgalar and the Tower of Azora in Elwynn Forest. While the true identity of Ilgalar remains shrouded in mystery, the two towers stand in quiet, magical opposition. In Elwynn, the Kirin Tor mage Theocritus maintains careful watch over Ilgalar through enchanted scrying devices like the Eye of Azora, monitoring Morganth's sinister experiments and plotting to neutralize the renegade before his corruption spreads further through the kingdom.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tower-of-ilgalar-6f6b1f9e2c58dda18a57/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.6889796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:trade-district"] = {
  hash = "ba5adf",
  fullHash = "ccf0a6",
  text = "Trade District. The Trade District is the bustling commercial heart of Stormwind City, located immediately past the main gates and the Valley of Heroes. It serves as the primary hub for adventurers and citizens alike, housing the city's central bank, auction house, guild registry, and diverse merchant shops. Crossing beneath the stone arches from the Valley of Heroes brings travelers directly into the Trade District. This vibrant open plaza acts as Stormwind's primary marketplace and logistical hub. At its center stands the Auction House, flanked nearby by the Stormwind Counting House, the city's main bank, and the popular tavern known as The Gilded Rose. The district directly bridges the gap between the military enclave of Old Town, the serene Cathedral Square, and the arcane avenues of the Mage Quarter, ensuring that coin, raw goods, and adventuring supplies circulate swiftly through the realm. Every essential commodity can be acquired along the paved avenues and flanking canals. Weapon smiths at Weller's Arsenal and Lionheart Armory supply blades and mail, fletchers peddle bows at The Empty Quiver, and artisanal stalls offer everything from fresh flowers to fine aged vintages from the Gallina Winery. Just inside the gates, the Stormwind Visitor's Center coordinates newly registered charters and tabard designs for organized adventuring guilds, while an elevated ramp on the eastern side climbs toward the Gryphon Roost, linking the capital's mercantile life to the rest of the Alliance territories.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_trade-district-d23e1e75cc55c812b354/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.5289796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tradesmen-s-terrace"] = {
  hash = "c5ee29",
  fullHash = "c44796",
  text = "Tradesmen's Terrace. Tradesmen's Terrace is the bustling commercial heart of Darnassus, situated along the city's southern flank. It brings together craftspeople, merchants, and the local auction house beneath the sweeping wooden architecture of the night elf capital. The terrace serves as the primary trade link connecting artisans with adventurers journeying across Teldrassil and beyond. Occupying the southern stretch of Darnassus, Tradesmen's Terrace houses the capital's mercantile life. While much of the city is devoted to quiet contemplation, wilderness sanctuaries, and spiritual duties, this terrace brings together shopkeepers, tradespeople, and merchants serving both local kaldorei and travelers from the wider Alliance. It contains Darnassus's central auction house, general provisions, and crafting supply stores, making it the essential staging ground for journeys into the wilds. Broad footpaths connect it northward directly to the Warrior's Terrace and westward toward the serene waters of the Temple Gardens and the grand Temple of the Moon. Kaldorei crafts prioritize harmony with nature, and the shops here reflect that delicate balance. Even amidst everyday commerce, the terrace operates without the roaring furnaces or smog seen in dwarven or human workshops, favoring quiet stone, curated timber, and woven goods. For visiting adventurers seeking trade contracts, repairs, or materials gathered from Teldrassil's dense boughs, the terrace stands as the principal center of business in northern Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tradesmen-s-terrace-e1d1729518b89db7546c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.0963265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-heroes"] = {
  hash = "7d9bf7",
  fullHash = "193848",
  text = "Valley of Heroes. The Valley of Heroes forms the monumental southern entrance to Stormwind City, carrying travellers across a stone bridge spanning the moat. Flanked by towering statues honoring the leaders of the Alliance Expedition to Draenor, it serves as both an awe-inspiring welcome and a heavily fortified checkpoint. Erected during the grand rebuilding of Stormwind following the Second War, the statues lining the causeway commemorate the commanders of the Alliance Expedition. These champions marched through the Dark Portal into the orcish homeworld of Draenor to seal the rift forever, giving their lives so Azeroth might survive. Visitors proceeding north toward the Trade District pass beneath the visages of Archmage Khadgar, Kurdran Wildhammer, Ranger-Captain Alleria Windrunner, Force Commander Danath Trollbane, and High General Turalyon. Beyond its ceremonial role, the valley is the primary defensive choke point guarding Stormwind from overland invasion. The approaches are overseen by General Marcus Jonathan, commander of the city guard, who monitors all traffic entering and departing through the great gates astride his steed. Flanking commanders, including Major Mattingly and Field Marshal Stonebridge, maintain vigilance alongside rows of seasoned guards, ensuring the capital remains secure against external threats.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-heroes-03d6c95845e0d17b0b43/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.2032653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-kings"] = {
  hash = "95a679",
  fullHash = "fcca05",
  text = "Valley of Kings. The Valley of Kings is a lush canyon nestled in the southwestern corner of Loch Modan, directly south of the passage to Dun Morogh. It serves as a heavily watched gateway into the fiery Searing Gorge, flanked by monumental stone figures carved in memory of the War of the Three Hammers. Travelers venturing here walk beneath ancient dwarven history while facing the immediate threat of Blackrock encroachers and troggs. The valley's most commanding feature is the pair of colossal statues depicting King Madoran Bronzebeard and High Thane Khardros Wildhammer. Carved by joint commission from their sons following their deaths, these stone titans overlook the pass into the scorched southlands. They stand as a lasting monument to the allied victory in the War of the Three Hammers and an eternal warning against the Dark Iron clan, reminding all who look south of the ruin brought upon the Redridge Mountains by the summoning of Ragnaros. Beyond serving as a path between South Gate Pass and Stonesplinter Valley, the Valley of Kings holds the heavy iron gate leading down into the Searing Gorge. Because of the hostile Blackrock orcs, Dark Iron dwarves, and volcanic hazards lurking on the other side, the gate is kept strictly locked and guarded by Ironforge mountaineers. Mountaineer Pebblebitty keeps watch over the threshold, turning away all but the hardiest adventurers who can prove they possess the strength to survive the inferno beyond.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-kings-57fc0d547fc6b464ffc7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.3420408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:vault-of-ironforge"] = {
  hash = "4ad602",
  fullHash = "d6c4d5",
  text = "Vault of Ironforge. The Vault of Ironforge is the primary banking house and treasure repository for the dwarven capital. Situated in the bustling Commons directly opposite the Auction House, it serves as a central hub where citizens and travelers across the Alliance securely store their wealth. The vault's operations are overseen by Dinita and the esteemed Stonemantle family. Carved deep into the mountain rock of the Commons, the Vault of Ironforge stands as a symbol of dwarven fiscal security and craft. With the city's trade booming, travelers, soldiers, and merchants gather here daily to deposit coin, relics, and provisions under the vigilant eyes of city guards and tellers. Overseen by Dinita and the trusted Stonemantle family, the institution offers Alliance patrons absolute peace of mind.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_vault-of-ironforge-4bcc082290957484c54e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.2669388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:vul-gol-ogre-mound"] = {
  hash = "06cb61",
  fullHash = "d1fe4e",
  text = "Vul'Gol Ogre Mound. Vul'Gol Ogre Mound is a sunken basin in southern Duskwood overrun by the Splinter Fist ogres under the brute Zzarc'Vul. Once a cluster of human homesteads, its ruined farmhouses and subterranean cavern are now littered with the carcasses and scavenged plunder of the clan. Tucked away in the shadows southwest of the fork heading toward Stranglethorn Vale, the Vul'Gol Ogre Mound serves as the seat of the Splinter Fist clan in Duskwood. The basin was once peaceful farmland, but its human inhabitants have long since fled or perished, leaving behind hollowed-out cabins now claimed by lumbering ogres and used as crude shelters. Inside the cavern beneath the rise, piles of slaughtered black bears attest to the clan's voracious appetite. Their chieftain, Zzarc'Vul, rules over the encampment, hoarding odd trinkets alongside mundane scavenged goods. Because the beleaguered Night Watch must stretch its thin ranks to guard Darkshire against rabid worgen and the restless dead, Commander Althea Ebonlocke and her militia rely on outside mercenaries to cull the Splinter Fist numbers before they push north. However, the ogres have made enemies beyond the town guard. They raided the recluse Abercrombie, stealing a crate of his alchemical tools, and attracted the attention of inquisitive mages seeking the arcane lenses that chieftain Zzarc'Vul is rumored to possess.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_vul-gol-ogre-mound-e4d8d9a62c06df2947fb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.9053061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:war-room"] = {
  hash = "7c707f",
  fullHash = "961601",
  text = "War Room. Tucked deep inside Stormwind Keep, the War Room serves as the strategic nerve center of the Alliance military. Ringed with armaments, historical war tomes, and tactical charts, it is where generals and strategists monitor distant fronts and coordinate the defense of the kingdom. Branching off from the grand chambers of Stormwind Keep, the War Room is outfitted specifically for military logistics and high-level campaign planning. Its stone walls are decorated with rows of armaments, including swords, blunderbusses, and even heavy artillery cannons. Piles of military treatises and historical chronicles—such as accounts of the Second War, the Fall of Stormwind, and the Battle of Grim Batol—stand stacked on nearby tables. At the room's core lies a large tactical map table, allowing commanders to assess Alliance deployments across Azeroth while the royal court handles matters of state just steps away.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_war-room-be5b34ec736252ef3185/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.0661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:warsong-labor-camp"] = {
  hash = "c0acc1",
  fullHash = "bcc159",
  text = "Warsong Labor Camp. The Warsong Labor Camp is an isolated Horde timber-harvesting outpost tucked away in southeastern Ashenvale. Operated by Warsong loggers and defended by orcish sentries, the work site cuts lumber to fuel the growth of Durotar while braving the hostility of the surrounding forest. Situated east of Fallen Sky Lake and southwest of the Dor'Danil Barrow Den, the Warsong Labor Camp exists for one vital purpose: harvesting the dense timber of Ashenvale. The dry plains of Durotar offer little usable lumber to build cities like Orgrimmar, forcing the Warsong clan to push deep into the contested woods. Staffed by hardy loggers and orcish guards, the site operates in relative isolation, grinding through ancient trees despite the ever-present threat of night elf scouts and passing Alliance warbands. Because of its remote location far from the main road and fortified bases like Splintertree Post, the camp is exceptionally vulnerable. Alliance raiders often strike the working peons and watchmen, knowing reinforcements will not arrive swiftly. To the night elves, every felled tree is an act of sacrilege, turning this mundane logging camp into a perennial flashpoint in the regional war.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_warsong-labor-camp-0805feee075c80aa3e00/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.3967347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:westbrook-garrison"] = {
  hash = "75d6dc",
  fullHash = "44e21b",
  text = "Westbrook Garrison. Westbrook Garrison is a Stormwind Guard outpost situated on the western edge of Elwynn Forest near the Westfall border. Manned by an overworked, under-supplied skeleton crew under Deputy Rainer, it serves as the realm's first line of defense against the encroaching Riverpaw gnolls and rogue bandits. Positioned where Elwynn Forest begins to thin toward Westfall, Westbrook Garrison stands as a vital but struggling watchpoint. With much of the Stormwind Army committed abroad or tied down by political inertia in the capital, the outpost has been left critically shorthanded. Deputy Rainer and his handful of guards must patrol the western roads while fending off emboldened thieves and the vicious Riverpaw gnolls who have overrun the southern woods. Unable to secure formal reinforcements from Marshal Dughan in Goldshire, the garrison relies almost entirely on traveling adventurers to secure the highway. The primary threat facing Westbrook is the swelling gnoll presence centered around the south of Forest's Edge. The packs have grown bold enough to stage raids directly on supply lines and homesteads, organized under the fearsome gnoll chieftain known as Hogger. Outnumbered and overwhelmed by the brute strength of Hogger's forces, the garrison has posted a high bounty across its grounds, desperately seeking capable fighters willing to venture into the hills and bring down the beast before he pushes farther east.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_westbrook-garrison-1b2ef3ad44a172ba5307/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.3306122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:whelgar-s-excavation-site"] = {
  hash = "981de7",
  fullHash = "aa1d65",
  text = "Whelgar's Excavation Site. Whelgar's Excavation Site is a dwarven archaeological dig in the western foothills of the Wetlands. Intended to unearth ancient Titan relics and prehistoric secrets, the expedition ended in disaster when vicious mottled raptors overran the quarry. The surviving excavators are now trapped on a ridge overlooking the dig, cut off from their findings. Spearheaded by dwarven prospectors searching for ancient fossils and earthen mysteries, the excavation carved out a massive quarry between Menethil Harbor and Dun Modr. However, unearthing prehistoric remnants drew the wrath of native mottled raptors. Overwhelmed and under-equipped, the archaeologists retreated to a ridge overlooking the dig site, leaving their tents, tools, and unearthed relics scattered among prowling predators.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_whelgar-s-excavation-site-25eb59a2e567477bb27d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.2146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:windshear-crag"] = {
  hash = "647da1",
  fullHash = "aa54a1",
  text = "Windshear Crag. Windshear Crag is a heavily despoiled valley in the central Stonetalon Mountains, stripped of its ancient timber by the Venture Company. Once a lush forest, the region is now an industrial wasteland dominated by whirring shredders, murky runoff, and aggressive logging crews. In the shadows of the machinery, local kobolds burrow for ore while mountain passes connect the scarred landscape to Ashenvale. Not long ago, Windshear Crag was a thick, old-growth woodland flanking the slopes of Stonetalon. That tranquil wilderness vanished when the Venture Company arrived in search of profit. Today, the basin is filled with severed stumps, acrid smog, and clanking machinery. The entire logging enterprise is overseen by Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle, a leper gnome whose engineering genius produced the Super Reaper 6000—a mechanical terror capable of reducing massive trunks to kindling in moments. From an elevated platform above the polluted Cragpool Lake, Gerenzo directs an unchecked operation that strips the mountain bare, earning the hostility of rival engineers and local inhabitants alike. Beyond the active timberyards, Windshear Crag holds other hazards and crossroads. To the southeast lies Windshear Mine, where territorial kobolds dig through the rock for Smoky Iron Ingots, fiercely attacking anyone who nears their haul. In the crag's northeast reaches, travelers can find the Talondeep Path, an underground cavern tunnel threading straight beneath the ridges into Ashenvale. Stationed near this shadowed portal stands the tauren elder Braug Dimspirit, who tests the historical wisdom and resolve of Horde wayfarers venturing through the contested peaks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_windshear-crag-aa06795d9b06b0b1c383/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.6571429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:yorgen-farmstead"] = {
  hash = "35943b",
  fullHash = "256950",
  text = "Yorgen Farmstead. The Yorgen Farmstead is a derelict farm nestled in southern Duskwood, fallen into ruin alongside the rest of the troubled region. Formerly owned by the farmer Sven Yorgen, the property has been seized and occupied by brigands of the Defias Brotherhood. Situated amidst the gloomy woodlands of southern Duskwood, the Yorgen Farmstead was once a functioning homestead belonging to Sven Yorgen. Like many isolated holdings in the region, the farm could not withstand the encroaching perils of the territory. Sven was forced to abandon his lands, retreating north to a makeshift camp along the Darkened Bank. In his absence, the abandoned grounds were claimed by outlaws looking for a foothold far from the gaze of Stormwind's authorities. Rather than lying vacant, the farmstead has been repurposed as a staging ground by the Defias Brotherhood. Masked highwaymen and stealthy cutthroats lurk among the decaying fences and weathered outbuildings, using the dense tree cover to conceal their presence. Travelers approaching the farmstead must tread carefully, as Defias assassins patrol the perimeter and ambush anyone trespassing on their newly seized sanctuary.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_yorgen-farmstead-c5d82600fd165e1b524d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.5755102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:zoram-gar-outpost"] = {
  hash = "30471b",
  fullHash = "228770",
  text = "Zoram'gar Outpost. Zoram'gar Outpost is a fortified Horde beachhead nestled on the western edge of Ashenvale along the Zoram Strand. Serving as a crucial coastal encampment, it provides the Horde with access to the Veiled Sea and keeps watch over regional threats like the encroaching naga and the sinister depths nearby. Situated at the northwestern lip of Ashenvale where the primeval forest collapses into the sea, Zoram'gar Outpost serves as the Horde's isolated coastal footing on the Zoram Strand. Hemmed in by night elf territory inland and maritime hazards offshore, the encampment acts as a staging ground for scouts, hunters, and shaman tasked with tracking local dangers. Life at the outpost is an endless vigil: naga regularly prowl the shallows, while lurking cultists and primal horrors pulse from the subterranean cavern system known as Blackfathom Deeps just down the shore. Because of its peripheral location, Zoram'gar maintains flight connections with other critical Horde holdings like Splintertree Post, the Crossroads, and Thunder Bluff. While small in footprint, the outpost anchors Horde supply lines on the western coast.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_zoram-gar-outpost-3e44b37e8f2d7ba78336/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.7542857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:zoram-strand"] = {
  hash = "7f56b6",
  fullHash = "4d9d06",
  text = "Zoram Strand. The Zoram Strand is a windswept coastline lining the northwestern edge of Ashenvale along the Veiled Sea. Once the site of an ancient night elven city swallowed by the Great Sundering, the shore is now plagued by Wrathtail naga, shipwrecks, and beached leviathans. It also holds the entrance to the submerged temple complex of Blackfathom Deeps. Ten thousand years ago, the grand night elven city of Zoram graced these shores before the Great Sundering tore Kalimdor apart and cast the settlement into the ocean depths. Today, the tide continually washes up ancient relics, remnants of shattered vessels, and the carcasses of massive sea beasts. The Wrathtail naga have overrun the beaches and tidal pools, fiercely guarding the flooded ruins and ancient artifacts against anyone daring to approach. Despite the naga menace, both mainland travelers and coastal wanderers find themselves drawn to the strand. To the south lies the fortified Horde encampment of Zoram'gar Outpost, whose warriors fight a continuous battle to secure the coast. Deep within the nearby caverns sits Blackfathom Deeps, an ancient temple of Elune that has fallen into dark hands, making the strand a gathering ground for scouts, scholars, and treasure seekers braving the surf.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_zoram-strand-084fe6bfab3a31283bf3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.6734694, start = 0},
  },
}
